,"explanation":"Volume or salt depletion increases the first-dose hypotension risk."},{"id":"B","text":"A remote healed fracture without current symptoms","explanation":"This does not identify volume or salt depletion."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable ankle edema without reduced intake or fluid loss","explanation":"This does not identify the volume-depletion risk sought."},{"id":"D","text":"Stable glucose control without recent illness","explanation":"This does not identify depletion of fluid or salt."}],"point":"Recognize volume/salt depletion as a hypotension risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--comprehensive--losartan_volume_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_volume_risk"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which risk cluster can increase when another RAAS blocker is combined with losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hypotension, hyperkalemia and improved renal function","explanation":"Renal impairment is an added risk; improvement is not guaranteed."},{"id":"B","text":"Hypotension, hyperkalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Dual RAAS blockade can increase all three adverse outcomes."},{"id":"C","text":"Hypertension, hyperkalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Additive RAAS blockade increases hypotension rather than hypertension risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Hypotension, hypokalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Dual blockade can raise potassium rather than lower it."}],"point":"Recall the combined risks of dual RAAS blockade.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-losartan--comprehensive--losartan_dual_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_dual_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on losartan adds a potassium supplement and an NSAID. Which paired risks need review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hyperkalemia and deterioration of renal function","explanation":"The supplement can add potassium risk; the NSAID can worsen renal function."},{"id":"B","text":"Hypokalemia and deterioration of renal function","explanation":"Potassium supplements add hyperkalemia, not hypokalemia, risk."},{"id":"C","text":"Hyperkalemia and isolated hepatic injury","explanation":"NSAID interaction concern is renal deterioration rather than isolated hepatic injury."},{"id":"D","text":"Hypokalemia and isolated hepatic injury","explanation":"Both the potassium direction and organ-specific interaction are incorrect."}],"answerKey":"A","point":"Associate potassium-raising drugs and NSAIDs with their specific risks.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-losartan--comprehensive--raas_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::raas_interactions"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Losartan is on the medication chart. Which newly prescribed antihypertensive also blocks the renin–angiotensin system?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hydralazine","explanation":"Hydralazine is a direct vasodilator, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"B","text":"Amlodipine","explanation":"Amlodipine is a calcium-channel blocker, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"C","text":"Bisoprolol","explanation":"Bisoprolol is a beta blocker, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"D","text":"Enalapril","explanation":"Enalapril is an ACE inhibitor and also acts on the renin–angiotensin system; this combination needs review."}],"point":"Recognize the need to review concurrent renin-angiotensin system blockers.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--comprehensive--losartan_dual_raas","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_dual_raas"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which paired change follows from the AT1-receptor blockade for losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both are downstream effects of blocking angiotensin II at AT1 receptors."},{"id":"B","text":"Less vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"The vascular direction fits, but the aldosterone direction does not."},{"id":"C","text":"More vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"The aldosterone direction fits, but the vascular direction does not."},{"id":"D","text":"More vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both directions oppose the consequences of AT1 blockade."}],"point":"Relate AT1 blockade to reduced vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--comprehensive--losartan_at1_effects","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_at1_effects"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":13,"correctionRevision":"bp-author-review-20261107","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-verified-losartan","title":"LOSARTAN POTASSIUM TABLET [UNICHEM PHARMACEUTICALS (USA), INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=92691768-b5e6-4527-98dc-d86c58f6f13e"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB).","mechanism":"Selectively blocks angiotensin II binding to the AT1 receptor, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects.","use":"Main use: treatment of hypertension. Current labeling also includes selected hypertensive patients with left ventricular hypertrophy and proteinuric diabetic nephropathy in type 2 diabetes with a history of hypertension.","administration":"Oral tablet. The Palestinian Essential Medicines List identifies a 50 mg tablet. Administer only according to the prescribed order; verify the product/strength in hand and current local protocol.","monitor":"• Check blood pressure and watch for symptomatic hypotension, especially with volume or salt depletion. • Follow creatinine/renal function and serum potassium: renal deterioration and hyperkalemia can occur. • Potassium-raising drugs increase hyperkalemia risk; NSAIDs can worsen renal function. • Lithium concentrations and toxicity can increase; monitor serum lithium with concurrent use. • Other renin-angiotensin system blockers can add hypotension, hyperkalemia and renal-impairment risk. • Pregnancy carries fetal-toxicity risk: discontinue losartan promptly and arrange an alternative prescribing plan.","why":"Blocking angiotensin II AT1-receptor effects reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone-mediated effects, lowering blood pressure. Renal function and potassium matter because the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system also helps regulate renal perfusion and potassium balance.","availability":"Palestinian-system evidence: the Palestinian Essential Medicines List includes losartan 50 mg tablet as an antihypertensive. Current stock/availability at a specific Gaza facility has not been independently confirmed. ED shelf/drawer label photographed, 2 Nov 2026. This is a named compartment, not an independently audited stock count; check the package before use."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB).","id":"section-0-3f1fae5eb01a2edd"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Selectively blocks angiotensin II binding to the AT1 receptor, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects.","id":"section-1-e0590ea2f88e8a84"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Main use: treatment of hypertension. Current labeling also includes selected hypertensive patients with left ventricular hypertrophy and proteinuric diabetic nephropathy in type 2 diabetes with a history of hypertension.","id":"section-2-167d881578a81154"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Oral tablet. The Palestinian Essential Medicines List identifies a 50 mg tablet. Administer only according to the prescribed order; verify the product/strength in hand and current local protocol.","id":"section-3-211952c68f0127a4"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check blood pressure and watch for symptomatic hypotension, especially with volume or salt depletion. • Follow creatinine/renal function and serum potassium: renal deterioration and hyperkalemia can occur. • Potassium-raising drugs increase hyperkalemia risk; NSAIDs can worsen renal function. • Lithium concentrations and toxicity can increase; monitor serum lithium with concurrent use. • Other renin-angiotensin system blockers can add hypotension, hyperkalemia and renal-impairment risk. • Pregnancy carries fetal-toxicity risk: discontinue losartan promptly and arrange an alternative prescribing plan.","id":"section-4-61e1dd646efdb6b3"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Blocking angiotensin II AT1-receptor effects reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone-mediated effects, lowering blood pressure. Renal function and potassium matter because the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system also helps regulate renal perfusion and potassium balance.","id":"section-5-72b0d753cedd2338"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"Palestinian-system evidence: the Palestinian Essential Medicines List includes losartan 50 mg tablet as an antihypertensive. Current stock/availability at a specific Gaza facility has not been independently confirmed. ED shelf/drawer label photographed, 2 Nov 2026. This is a named compartment, not an independently audited stock count; check the package before use.","id":"section-6-9b22fa9b70cd8914"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"verified-losartan--standard","itemId":"verified-losartan","itemType":"medication","title":"LOSARTAN","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswer