r, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"D","text":"Bisoprolol","explanation":"Bisoprolol is a beta blocker, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."}],"point":"Recognize the need to review concurrent renin-angiotensin system blockers.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--advanced--losartan_dual_raas","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_dual_raas"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which paired change follows from the AT1-receptor blockade for losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Less vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"The vascular direction fits, but the aldosterone direction does not."},{"id":"B","text":"More vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"The aldosterone direction fits, but the vascular direction does not."},{"id":"C","text":"More vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both directions oppose the consequences of AT1 blockade."},{"id":"D","text":"Less vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both are downstream effects of blocking angiotensin II at AT1 receptors."}],"point":"Relate AT1 blockade to reduced vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--advanced--losartan_at1_effects","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_at1_effects"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"verified-losartan--7-day","itemId":"verified-losartan","itemType":"medication","title":"LOSARTAN","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"comprehensive memory retest; questions may repeat","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of LOSARTAN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Blocks angiotensin-II AT1 receptors","explanation":"AT1 blockade reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits hepatic HMG-CoA reductase","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: AT1 blockade reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S subunit","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: AT1 blockade reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Suppresses gastric H/K ATPase","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: AT1 blockade reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::principal-action"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--principal-action","answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does LOSARTAN belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dihydropyridine CCB","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)."},{"id":"B","text":"Beta1 blocker","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)."},{"id":"C","text":"ARB","explanation":"Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)."},{"id":"D","text":"Uroselective alpha-1 blocker","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB)."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::drug-class"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--drug-class","answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of LOSARTAN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Treatment of hypertension","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Main use: treatment of hypertension. Current labeling also includes selected hypertensive patients with left ventricular hypertrophy and proteinuric diabetic nephropathy in type 2 diabetes with a history of hypertension."},{"id":"C","text":"Acute reversal of opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement of circulating glucose in severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::treatment-role"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--treatment-role","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient becomes light-headed on standing after LOSARTAN. Which paired assessment best checks the suspected adverse effect?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Resting temperature and infection symptoms","explanation":"These investigate infection rather than the suspected pressure effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Bowel frequency and abdominal distension","explanation":"These investigate a gastrointestinal effect rather than postural circulatory tolerance."},{"id":"C","text":"Visual acuity and accommodation symptoms","explanation":"These evaluate visual effects rather than establish a postural pressure fall."},{"id":"D","text":"Postural BP and associated symptoms","explanation":"A positional pressure fall can account for the light-headedness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize symptomatic hypotension during treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--hypotension","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::hypotension"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--hypotension","answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which trend is most directly relevant to the renal safety monitoring described for LOSARTAN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Creatinine and urine-output trends","explanation":"These assess kidney function and renal perfusion in clinical context."},{"id":"B","text":"Temperature and neutrophil-count trends","explanation":"These primarily assess infection or blood-cell changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Troponin and chest-pain trends","explanation":"These assess myocardial injury rather than directly establish renal status."},{"id":"D","text":"INR and puncture-site bleeding trends","explanation":"These assess anticoagulation and bleeding rather than renal function."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor renal function when kidney injury or altered treatment safety is relevant.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--renal_monitor","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::renal_monitor"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--renal_monitor","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which electrolyte trend is a particular concern after starting LOSARTAN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rising serum potassium","explanation":"The treatment can increase potassium or reduce its loss."},{"id":"B","text":"Falling serum potassium","explanation":"This is the opposite of the characteristic potassium-raising concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Falling serum phosphate","explanation":"Phosphate depletion is not the principal effect being tested."},{"id":"D","text":"Rising serum calcium","explanation":"Calcium elevation is not the principal effect being tested."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Monitor for hyperkalemia with potassium-raising treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--high_k","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::high_k"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--high_k","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient taking LOSARTAN reports pregnancy. Which risk is the main reason to review the prescription promptly?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Maternal hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is not the characteristic pregnancy-specific concern for this treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"Maternal ototoxicity","explanation":"Hearing injury is not the pregnancy warning being tested."},{"id":"C","text":"Fetal opioid withdrawal","explanation":"This treatment is not an opioid."},{"id":"D","text":"Fetal toxicity","explanation":"Pregnancy is a major warning for the RAAS-blocking treatment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize pregnancy as a major safety concern with RAAS-blocking therapy.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--pregnancy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::pregnancy"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--pregnancy","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Besides routine hypertension treatment, in which pair of conditions may losartan also be indicated?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Symptomatic bradycardia; complete heart block","explanation":"These are conduction problems, not the additional uses associated with losartan."},{"id":"B","text":"Hypertension with LVH; nephropathy in type 2 diabetes","explanation":"Losartan may be indicated in selected hypertensive patients with LVH or nephropathy in type 2 diabetes; these are not universal indications for everyone with diabetes or LVH."},{"id":"C","text":"Ventricular tachycardia; atrial fibrillation","explanation":"Losartan is not used as an antiarrhythmic for these rhythms."},{"id":"D","text":"Acute MI chest pain; acute cardiogenic shock","explanation":"These are acute syndromes requiring their own treatment; they are not the additional uses applicable here."}],"point":"Recognize the selected LVH and type 2 diabetic nephropathy uses taught in addition to hypertension.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_additional_uses","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_additional_uses"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--losartan_additional_uses","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Besides potassium-raising drugs and NSAIDs, which medicine requires interaction review with losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Loratadine","explanation":"Loratadine is not the separately named interaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Levothyroxine","explanation":"Levothyroxine is not the separately named interaction."},{"id":"C","text":"Lithium","explanation":"Lithium is explicitly listed for interaction review; the product label describes increased lithium exposure/toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"Lactulose","explanation":"Lactulose is not the separately named interaction."}],"point":"Recognize lithium as a medicine requiring interaction review with losartan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_lithium","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_lithium"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--losartan_lithium","answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pretreatment history increases the risk of symptomatic hypotension when losartan is started?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Stable glucose control without recent illness","explanation":"This does not identify depletion of fluid or salt."},{"id":"B","text":"Recent vomiting with reduced intake and volume depletion","explanation":"Volume or salt depletion increases the first-dose hypotension risk."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable ankle edema without reduced intake or fluid loss","explanation":"This does not identify the volume-depletion risk sought."},{"id":"D","text":"A remote healed fracture without current symptoms","explanation":"This does not identify volume or salt depletion."}],"point":"Recognize volume/salt depletion as a hypotension risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_volume_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_volume_risk"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--losartan_volume_risk","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which risk cluster can increase when another RAAS blocker is combined with losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hypotension, hyperkalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Dual RAAS blockade can increase all three adverse outcomes."},{"id":"B","text":"Hypotension, hypokalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Dual blockade can raise potassium rather than lower it."},{"id":"C","text":"Hypotension, hyperkalemia and improved renal function","explanation":"Renal impairment is an added risk; improvement is not guaranteed."},{"id":"D","text":"Hypertension, hyperkalemia and renal impairment","explanation":"Additive RAAS blockade increases hypotension rather than hypertension risk."}],"point":"Recall the combined risks of dual RAAS blockade.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_dual_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_dual_risk"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--standard--losartan_dual_risk","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient on LOSARTAN has newly added potassium supplements and an NSAID. Which review best addresses the interaction risks?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR, vitamin-K intake, and the anticoagulant target","explanation":"No warfarin exposure or INR-based indication is described."},{"id":"B","text":"Hemoglobin, stool pattern, and laxative timing","explanation":"These do not address the principal RAAS-related combination risk in the case."},{"id":"C","text":"QT interval, magnesium, and inhaler technique","explanation":"These are not the principal monitoring domains established by this combination."},{"id":"D","text":"Potassium, renal function, and the interacting medicines","explanation":"Both potassium-raising exposure and NSAID-related renal risk are relevant."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review potassium-raising medicines and NSAIDs with the potassium and renal-function checks tested in this question.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--raas_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::raas_interactions"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--advanced--raas_interactions","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Losartan is on the medication chart. Which newly prescribed antihypertensive also blocks the renin–angiotensin system?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hydralazine","explanation":"Hydralazine is a direct vasodilator, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"B","text":"Bisoprolol","explanation":"Bisoprolol is a beta blocker, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"C","text":"Amlodipine","explanation":"Amlodipine is a calcium-channel blocker, not another renin–angiotensin system blocker."},{"id":"D","text":"Enalapril","explanation":"Enalapril is an ACE inhibitor and also acts on the renin–angiotensin system; this combination needs review."}],"point":"Recognize the need to review concurrent renin-angiotensin system blockers.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_dual_raas","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_dual_raas"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--advanced--losartan_dual_raas","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which paired change follows from the AT1-receptor blockade for losartan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"More vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"The aldosterone direction fits, but the vascular direction does not."},{"id":"B","text":"Less vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"The vascular direction fits, but the aldosterone direction does not."},{"id":"C","text":"Less vasoconstriction and less aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both are downstream effects of blocking angiotensin II at AT1 receptors."},{"id":"D","text":"More vasoconstriction and more aldosterone effect","explanation":"Both directions oppose the consequences of AT1 blockade."}],"point":"Relate AT1 blockade to reduced vasoconstriction and aldosterone effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"verified-losartan--7-day--losartan_at1_effects","points":1,"learningPointIds":["verified-losartan::losartan_at1_effects"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"verified-losartan--advanced--losartan_at1_effects","answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":14}],"scoringExclusions":[{"learningPointId":"verified-losartan::hypotension","action":"omit","reason":"Context-free shared template: the answer is supplied by the symptom or asks for a general product-reference rule. Omit from drug-specific scoring; do not credit this as coverage of the underlying drug-specific administration or BP fact.","unresolvedCoverage":true}]},{"itemId":"ibd2-acamol","title":"ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen)","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive","itemId":"ibd2-acamol","itemType":"medication","title":"ACAMOL — paracetamol (acetaminophen)","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which mechanism best fits the predominant analgesic and antipyretic effects of paracetamol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Predominantly central inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis","explanation":"Central prostaglandin signaling is implicated in paracetamol’s analgesia and antipyresis."},{"id":"B","text":"Predominantly peripheral irreversible inhibition of platelet COX-1","explanation":"That describes aspirin’s platelet action rather than paracetamol’s primary effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Central activation of mu-opioid receptors","explanation":"Paracetamol does not act as a standard mu-opioid agonist."},{"id":"D","text":"Predominantly peripheral inhibition of prostaglandins at inflamed joints","explanation":"This better fits the peripheral anti-inflammatory activity of NSAIDs."}],"point":"Identify its predominantly central prostaglandin-related action.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which therapeutic classification best fits paracetamol in a postoperative medication chart?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Non-opioid NSAID used primarily for peripheral anti-inflammatory effect","explanation":"Its therapeutic profile differs from anti-inflammatory NSAIDs."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-opioid analgesic and antipyretic with little peripheral anti-inflammatory effect","explanation":"Paracetamol is used for analgesia and fever reduction rather than the typical peripheral anti-inflammatory effects of NSAIDs."},{"id":"C","text":"Centrally acting opioid used mainly for analgesia","explanation":"Paracetamol is centrally acting but is not an opioid."},{"id":"D","text":"Opioid antagonist used mainly for reversing opioid effects","explanation":"Paracetamol does not reverse opioid toxicity."}],"point":"Distinguish a non-opioid analgesic and antipyretic from adjacent drug classes.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"On an orthopaedic ward, a patient has mild-to-moderate pain after fracture fixation. Which role fits prescribed paracetamol in a multimodal plan?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A targeted peripheral anti-inflammatory substitute with the same effect as an NSAID","explanation":"Its peripheral anti-inflammatory effect is limited compared with usual NSAIDs."},{"id":"B","text":"An opioid rescue dose for breakthrough pain","explanation":"Paracetamol is not an opioid and does not replace the indicated rescue plan."},{"id":"C","text":"Scheduled non-opioid analgesia alongside the other indicated measures","explanation":"Paracetamol can provide a non-opioid component of postoperative or fracture pain control."},{"id":"D","text":"A local anesthetic delivered at the fracture site","explanation":"Paracetamol is not a local anesthetic."}],"point":"Choose an adjunct for mild-to-moderate pain within multimodal therapy.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"numeric-entry","stem":"At 18:00, before a pending dose, the chart shows 500 mg paracetamol yesterday at 16:00, two 500-mg tablets today at 07:00, 1,000 mg IV acetaminophen at 11:00, and two combination tablets at 15:00 that each contain 325 mg acetaminophen. How many milligrams of paracetamol/acetaminophen has the patient already received in the preceding 24 hours?","answer":2650,"unit":"mg","tolerance":0,"explanation":"Count 1,000 mg oral + 1,000 mg IV + 650 mg in the combination tablets. The dose at 16:00 yesterday falls outside the 24-hour window. Compare the cumulative exposure and pending dose with the patient-specific order and limit.","point":"Calculate rolling 24-hour exposure from all formulations and routes.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--acetaminophen_total","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetaminophen_total"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A dosing reconciliation finds a potentially toxic paracetamol exposure. The patient feels well several hours after the last dose. Which interpretation should guide escalation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Hepatic injury would already cause jaundice, so reassess tomorrow before escalating","explanation":"Clinical signs may be delayed. Waiting for jaundice can delay overdose treatment."},{"id":"B","text":"There is mainly an immediate platelet effect, so wait for a platelet count","explanation":"Platelet count does not establish the risk of delayed acetaminophen-induced liver injury."},{"id":"C","text":"Hepatic injury may become evident later, so escalate the suspected overdose now","explanation":"Early symptoms may be absent even when the exposure can cause serious hepatic injury."},{"id":"D","text":"The main issue is delayed NSAID-type GI bleeding, so monitor stool alone","explanation":"Excess paracetamol is principally a hepatotoxicity concern. Stool surveillance cannot replace urgent assessment."}],"point":"Recognize that serious liver injury can be delayed despite few early symptoms.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--hepatic_overdose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::hepatic_overdose"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Before oral paracetamol, an orthopaedic patient reports 2/10 pain at rest and 6/10 during transfer. Which reassessment supports an analgesic response for the documented activity?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Transfer pain falls from 6/10 to 3/10 at a comparable level of assistance","explanation":"A comparable transfer directly assesses the patient’s documented movement pain."},{"id":"B","text":"Resting pain after the dose is 2/10, compared with 6/10 during transfer before it","explanation":"Resting and movement scores are not directly comparable."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain is 3/10 with passive movement, but transfer is not repeated","explanation":"This uses a different activity and does not establish the response for transfer."},{"id":"D","text":"Transfer pain stays at 6/10, while temperature falls","explanation":"The temperature response does not establish a benefit for movement pain."}],"point":"Compare pain on the same activity before and after the dose.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::pain_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After ordered paracetamol in a febrile patient, temperature falls from 39.1°C to 37.9°C while other infection findings persist. Which interpretation is appropriate?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The temperature trend establishes resolution of infection","explanation":"An antipyretic response does not prove that infection has resolved."},{"id":"B","text":"The temperature trend establishes adequate analgesia","explanation":"Temperature does not measure pain relief."},{"id":"C","text":"The temperature trend indicates an anti-inflammatory effect equivalent to an NSAID","explanation":"Reduced fever alone does not establish the peripheral anti-inflammatory action of NSAIDs."},{"id":"D","text":"The temperature trend is consistent with antipyresis, and the cause still needs assessment","explanation":"Paracetamol can reduce fever without treating its underlying cause."}],"point":"Use measured temperature to assess symptomatic antipyresis without inferring resolution of the cause.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--acetam_fever_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetam_fever_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"When comparing oral paracetamol with a conventional NSAID for mild orthopaedic pain, which trade-off matters for a patient with bleeding concerns?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Paracetamol has the same platelet and GI effects as a conventional NSAID, but no cumulative liver toxicity","explanation":"This misses its different adverse-effect profile and hepatic overdose risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Paracetamol has the same irreversible platelet inhibition as aspirin and less liver toxicity at any dose","explanation":"It does not reproduce aspirin’s irreversible platelet inhibition. Excess paracetamol can injure the liver."},{"id":"C","text":"Paracetamol provides stronger peripheral anti-inflammatory action and equivalent GI bleeding risk","explanation":"It is used chiefly for pain and fever rather than stronger peripheral anti-inflammatory action."},{"id":"D","text":"Paracetamol has less of the typical NSAID platelet and GI effect, but excess dosing can injure the liver","explanation":"Paracetamol provides analgesia without the usual NSAID platelet/GI profile. Its total exposure still needs review."}],"point":"Distinguish usual NSAID platelet and GI effects from paracetamol’s exposure-related liver risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","id":"ibd2-acamol--comprehensive--acetam_nsaid_distinction","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acetam_nsaid_distinction"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history is most directly relevant to reassessing the hepatic safety of repeated paracetamol dosing?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Aspirin-exacerbated wheeze without liver disease or heavy alcohol use","explanation":"This is an important medication history, but does not identify the hepatic risk factors asked for."},{"id