ds review?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The assumption that combination-product acetaminophen need not count","explanation":"All sources contribute to total exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"The assumption that absence of pain eliminates hepatic risk","explanation":"Analgesic need does not determine hepatic safety."},{"id":"C","text":"The next dose based on the generic maximum without patient factors","explanation":"A universal maximum is not safe for every patient."},{"id":"D","text":"The patient-specific safe limit rather than the generic maximum alone","explanation":"Frailty and liver disease may lower the appropriate limit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review the patient-specific acetaminophen limit in frailty and liver disease.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-acamol--7-day--acen_patient_limit","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-acamol::acen_patient_limit"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-acamol--advanced--acen_patient_limit","answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":10}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ibd2-trufen","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive","itemId":"ibd2-trufen","itemType":"medication","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which enzyme effect explains both ibuprofen’s anti-inflammatory action and its effect on protective prostaglandins?","point":"Link reversible COX inhibition to lower inflammatory, gastric and renal prostaglandins.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversible platelet COX inhibition lowers thromboxane without affecting other prostaglandins","explanation":"Ibuprofen reversibly inhibits COX and its effects are not limited to platelet thromboxane."},{"id":"B","text":"Reversible COX inhibition lowers inflammatory, gastric and renal prostaglandins","explanation":"COX inhibition contributes to symptom relief and can impair mucosal protection and renal perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Reversible COX inhibition raises gastric and renal prostaglandins","explanation":"Inhibiting COX reduces rather than increases production of these prostaglandins."},{"id":"D","text":"Reversible lipoxygenase inhibition lowers leukotrienes without affecting prostaglandins","explanation":"The relevant pathway for ibuprofen here is COX-dependent prostaglandin synthesis."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which therapeutic profile identifies ibuprofen in an orthopaedic medication review?","point":"Identify ibuprofen as an analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory NSAID.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Non-NSAID analgesic-antipyretic with little peripheral anti-inflammatory activity","explanation":"This more closely describes paracetamol."},{"id":"B","text":"Glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory with immunosuppressive activity","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a corticosteroid."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid analgesic with no direct antipyretic activity","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not an opioid."},{"id":"D","text":"NSAID with analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory activity","explanation":"All three actions are characteristic of ibuprofen."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After a soft-tissue injury, an adult has movement pain and localized inflammatory swelling. Which effect is the appropriate goal of an ordered ibuprofen course?","point":"Recognize relief of musculoskeletal pain and inflammatory swelling.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduce pain while leaving inflammatory swelling unchanged as an expected drug limitation","explanation":"This would miss the NSAID’s potential anti-inflammatory benefit."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduce muscle spasm through direct skeletal muscle relaxation","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a direct muscle relaxant."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduce both movement-related pain and localized inflammatory swelling","explanation":"Ibuprofen’s analgesic and anti-inflammatory effects fit the treatment goal."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduce edema by producing a diuretic effect","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a diuretic and can contribute to fluid retention."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult taking ibuprofen reports several new GI symptoms. Which pattern most strongly suggests upper GI bleeding?","point":"Distinguish melena and coffee-ground emesis from nonhemorrhagic symptoms.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Epigastric discomfort with clear emesis and unchanged stool","explanation":"Dyspepsia needs assessment but is less specific for overt bleeding."},{"id":"B","text":"Tarry stool and coffee-ground emesis","explanation":"Both can indicate upper GI blood loss and require timely escalation."},{"id":"C","text":"Bright red streaks on hard stool with no emesis","explanation":"This can be lower tract bleeding. The pattern is less suggestive of an upper source."},{"id":"D","text":"Dark formed stool after starting iron and a single episode of clear emesis","explanation":"Iron can darken stool. Tarry consistency or altered blood in emesis makes an upper bleed more likely."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_gi"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in a patient taking ibuprofen most strongly warrants reassessing renal risk before the next ordered dose?","point":"Identify volume depletion and nephrotoxic co-exposure as risks for NSAID-associated kidney injury.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Controlled nausea, normal intake and a prescribed paracetamol dose","explanation":"These findings do not identify the same renal perfusion or nephrotoxic risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Constipation, normal intake and stable creatinine","explanation":"This does not identify new volume depletion or deteriorating renal function."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeated vomiting, poor intake and concurrent gentamicin treatment","explanation":"Reduced effective volume makes renal perfusion more dependent on prostaglandins, and gentamicin adds nephrotoxic risk. Review renal function and the regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Improving mobility, stable urine output and a new oral iron prescription","explanation":"This does not identify the same renal risk pattern."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_renal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which prior reaction most directly changes the decision to give ibuprofen because of potential cross-reactivity?","point":"Identify aspirin or NSAID-associated bronchospasm as a hypersensitivity concern.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dyspepsia after taking naproxen","explanation":"This deserves GI risk review but does not by itself identify a respiratory hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Acute wheezing after taking aspirin","explanation":"Aspirin-associated bronchospasm can cross-react with nonselective NSAIDs such as ibuprofen. Escalate before administration."},{"id":"C","text":"Nausea after taking codeine","explanation":"This can be an opioid adverse effect rather than evidence of NSAID cross-reactivity."},{"id":"D","text":"Dry cough while taking enalapril","explanation":"An ACE inhibitor cough does not establish NSAID-associated bronchospasm."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_allergy"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After several doses of ibuprofen, which paired trend suggests the drug may be worsening fluid retention and BP control?","point":"Link sodium and water retention to edema and rising blood pressure.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New ankle edema with a postural BP fall after vomiting","explanation":"Edema needs assessment, but vomiting and postural hypotension suggest another concurrent volume problem."},{"id":"B","text":"Rising BP with weight loss and increased urine output","explanation":"BP needs review, but this pattern does not support simultaneous fluid retention."},{"id":"C","text":"Weight gain with new melena and a falling BP","explanation":"Possible bleeding and hemodynamic instability require evaluation. The BP trend does not fit the coupled retention and hypertension pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"New ankle edema with rising BP","explanation":"NSAIDs can promote fluid retention and worsen hypertension. Reassess volume status and the treatment plan."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_cv","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_cv"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The order calls for a full tablet of plain ibuprofen 200 mg by the oral route. The patient also receives scheduled paracetamol. Which verified package matches the order without adding an unprescribed ingredient?","point":"Distinguish the exact oral strength and plain formulation from the combination product.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Plain Trufen 200 mg containing ibuprofen alone","explanation":"This matches the oral tablet order and adds no paracetamol."},{"id":"B","text":"Trufen Extra containing 200 mg ibuprofen plus paracetamol","explanation":"The ibuprofen strength matches, but the extra paracetamol would add to the scheduled exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Plain Trufen 600 mg containing ibuprofen alone","explanation":"The active ingredient matches, but a full tablet would exceed the prescribed 200 mg."},{"id":"D","text":"An oral paracetamol tablet containing 500 mg paracetamol alone","explanation":"This does not provide the prescribed ibuprofen and adds paracetamol."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--trufen_extra","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::trufen_extra"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Before ibuprofen, a patient has 6/10 pain on a standardized ankle movement and visible localized swelling. Which comparison most directly measures the intended treatment response?","point":"Reassess pain under comparable movement and reassess swelling.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeat the same movement pain assessment and compare swelling at the same site","explanation":"Comparable baseline and follow-up measures assess both analgesic and anti-inflammatory response."},{"id":"B","text":"Compare resting pain afterward with baseline movement pain and reassess swelling","explanation":"The swelling comparison helps, but the pain measures are taken under different conditions."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeat movement pain but use pulse and BP alone to judge swelling","explanation":"The pain comparison is relevant. Vital signs do not directly measure local swelling."},{"id":"D","text":"Compare swelling with baseline but use the temperature trend to judge analgesia","explanation":"The local swelling comparison helps, but temperature does not measure pain relief."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::pain_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Apart from fluid retention and blood pressure changes, which serious cardiovascular event pair is covered by the NSAID boxed warning for ibuprofen?","point":"Identify myocardial infarction and stroke as thrombotic risks.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Myocardial infarction and stroke","explanation":"Ibuprofen carries a warning about serious cardiovascular thrombotic events including MI and stroke."},{"id":"B","text":"Sinus arrest and second-degree AV block","explanation":"The warning concerns thrombotic events rather than direct nodal suppression."},{"id":"C","text":"Atrial fibrillation and torsades de pointes","explanation":"This is not the pair named in the cardiovascular thrombotic warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Pericarditis and myocarditis","explanation":"These are inflammatory conditions, not the thrombotic outcomes the warning identifies."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--ibuprofen_cv_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::ibuprofen_cv_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult is due for ibuprofen after an injury. Which purchased product creates an unintended duplicate NSAID exposure?","point":"Apply class knowledge to identify a second NSAID.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choic