ue,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Myocardial infarction and stroke","explanation":"Ibuprofen carries a warning about serious cardiovascular thrombotic events including MI and stroke."},{"id":"B","text":"Sinus arrest and second-degree AV block","explanation":"The warning concerns thrombotic events rather than direct nodal suppression."},{"id":"C","text":"Atrial fibrillation and torsades de pointes","explanation":"This is not the pair named in the cardiovascular thrombotic warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Pericarditis and myocarditis","explanation":"These are inflammatory conditions, not the thrombotic outcomes the warning identifies."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--ibuprofen_cv_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::ibuprofen_cv_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult is due for ibuprofen after an injury. Which purchased product creates an unintended duplicate NSAID exposure?","point":"Apply class knowledge to identify a second NSAID.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Paracetamol taken for the same pain","explanation":"This is not a second NSAID, although cumulative paracetamol needs separate review."},{"id":"B","text":"Gabapentin prescribed for neuropathic pain","explanation":"Gabapentin has a different mechanism and does not create NSAID class duplication."},{"id":"C","text":"Tramadol prescribed for breakthrough pain","explanation":"Tramadol is not an NSAID. Its own risks need separate consideration."},{"id":"D","text":"Naproxen taken for the same pain","explanation":"Naproxen and ibuprofen both inhibit COX. Concurrent use can increase GI and renal risk."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::duplicate_nsaid"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient’s orthopaedic pain is controlled at the lower end of the prescribed ibuprofen dose range. Which principle should guide review of the remaining treatment?","point":"Apply the lowest effective ordered dose and shortest appropriate duration.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the lowest effective ordered dose and continue for a fixed course despite recovery","explanation":"The dose is appropriate but duration should be reviewed against response."},{"id":"B","text":"Use a higher ordered dose and shorten the course even when the lower dose is effective","explanation":"A short course does not require dose escalation when the lower dose meets the goal."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the lowest effective ordered dose and review whether a shorter course meets the clinical goal","explanation":"Limiting dose and duration can reduce cumulative NSAID risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Use a higher ordered dose for the original duration to prevent pain from returning","explanation":"Unnecessary escalation increases exposure without an established need."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--ibuprofen_minimum_exposure","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::ibuprofen_minimum_exposure"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]}],"questionCount":12,"correctionRevision":"ibuprofen-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ibd2-trufen--21-day","itemId":"ibd2-trufen","itemType":"medication","title":"TRUFEN — ibuprofen","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which enzyme effect explains both ibuprofen’s anti-inflammatory action and its effect on protective prostaglandins?","point":"Link reversible COX inhibition to lower inflammatory, gastric and renal prostaglandins.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Irreversible platelet COX inhibition lowers thromboxane without affecting other prostaglandins","explanation":"Ibuprofen reversibly inhibits COX and its effects are not limited to platelet thromboxane."},{"id":"B","text":"Reversible COX inhibition lowers inflammatory, gastric and renal prostaglandins","explanation":"COX inhibition contributes to symptom relief and can impair mucosal protection and renal perfusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Reversible COX inhibition raises gastric and renal prostaglandins","explanation":"Inhibiting COX reduces rather than increases production of these prostaglandins."},{"id":"D","text":"Reversible lipoxygenase inhibition lowers leukotrienes without affecting prostaglandins","explanation":"The relevant pathway for ibuprofen here is COX-dependent prostaglandin synthesis."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which therapeutic profile identifies ibuprofen in an orthopaedic medication review?","point":"Identify ibuprofen as an analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory NSAID.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Non-NSAID analgesic-antipyretic with little peripheral anti-inflammatory activity","explanation":"This more closely describes paracetamol."},{"id":"B","text":"Glucocorticoid anti-inflammatory with immunosuppressive activity","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a corticosteroid."},{"id":"C","text":"Opioid analgesic with no direct antipyretic activity","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not an opioid."},{"id":"D","text":"NSAID with analgesic, antipyretic and anti-inflammatory activity","explanation":"All three actions are characteristic of ibuprofen."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After a soft-tissue injury, an adult has movement pain and localized inflammatory swelling. Which effect is the appropriate goal of an ordered ibuprofen course?","point":"Recognize relief of musculoskeletal pain and inflammatory swelling.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduce pain while leaving inflammatory swelling unchanged as an expected drug limitation","explanation":"This would miss the NSAID’s potential anti-inflammatory benefit."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduce muscle spasm through direct skeletal muscle relaxation","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a direct muscle relaxant."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduce both movement-related pain and localized inflammatory swelling","explanation":"Ibuprofen’s analgesic and anti-inflammatory effects fit the treatment goal."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduce edema by producing a diuretic effect","explanation":"Ibuprofen is not a diuretic and can contribute to fluid retention."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"An adult taking ibuprofen reports several new GI symptoms. Which pattern most strongly suggests upper GI bleeding?","point":"Distinguish melena and coffee-ground emesis from nonhemorrhagic symptoms.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Epigastric discomfort with clear emesis and unchanged stool","explanation":"Dyspepsia needs assessment but is less specific for overt bleeding."},{"id":"B","text":"Tarry stool and coffee-ground emesis","explanation":"Both can indicate upper GI blood loss and require timely escalation."},{"id":"C","text":"Bright red streaks on hard stool with no emesis","explanation":"This can be lower tract bleeding. The pattern is less suggestive of an upper source."},{"id":"D","text":"Dark formed stool after starting iron and a single episode of clear emesis","explanation":"Iron can darken stool. Tarry consistency or altered blood in emesis makes an upper bleed more likely."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_gi"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in a patient taking ibuprofen most strongly warrants reassessing renal risk before the next ordered dose?","point":"Identify volume depletion and nephrotoxic co-exposure as risks for NSAID-associated kidney injury.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Controlled nausea, normal intake and a prescribed paracetamol dose","explanation":"These findings do not identify the same renal perfusion or nephrotoxic risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Constipation, normal intake and stable creatinine","explanation":"This does not identify new volume depletion or deteriorating renal function."},{"id":"C","text":"Repeated vomiting, poor intake and concurrent gentamicin treatment","explanation":"Reduced effective volume makes renal perfusion more dependent on prostaglandins, and gentamicin adds nephrotoxic risk. Review renal function and the regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Improving mobility, stable urine output and a new oral iron prescription","explanation":"This does not identify the same renal risk pattern."}],"id":"ibd2-trufen--comprehensive--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-trufen::nsaid_renal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which prior reaction most directly changes the decision to give 