get of protein-synthesis inhibitors, rather than cefazolin."},{"id":"C","text":"DNA supercoiling by bacterial gyrase","explanation":"Gyrase inhibition is characteristic of fluoroquinolones rather than cefazolin."},{"id":"D","text":"Folate synthesis at dihydropteroate synthase","explanation":"This is a sulfonamide target rather than a cephalosporin action."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which beta-lactam subgroup contains cefazolin?","point":"Distinguish the first-generation cephalosporin from nearby beta-lactam classes.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Second-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"Cefuroxime is an example in this group. Cefazolin is first generation."},{"id":"B","text":"First-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"Cefazolin is a parenteral first-generation cephalosporin."},{"id":"C","text":"Third-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"Ceftriaxone is an example in this group. Cefazolin has a different classification."},{"id":"D","text":"Antistaphylococcal penicillin","explanation":"Some clinical uses overlap, but cefazolin belongs to the cephalosporins."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pair of orthopaedic scenarios can each justify cefazolin under a separate indication-specific order?","point":"Distinguish surgical prophylaxis from treatment of a susceptible bacterial infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Perioperative prophylaxis for indicated prosthetic joint surgery and treatment of a susceptible bacterial bone infection","explanation":"The surgical indication is preventive. Treatment of an established infection depends on its susceptibility and the prescribed plan."},{"id":"B","text":"Perioperative prophylaxis for indicated prosthetic joint surgery and targeted treatment of a fungal bone infection","explanation":"Cefazolin can be used for surgical prophylaxis but is not an antifungal."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of a susceptible bacterial bone infection and routine prophylaxis whenever a peripheral cannula is placed","explanation":"Cefazolin may treat a susceptible infection, but ordinary peripheral cannulation is not a routine indication for systemic antibiotics."},{"id":"D","text":"Targeted treatment of a fungal bone infection and routine prophylaxis whenever a peripheral cannula is placed","explanation":"Neither scenario fits the cefazolin indications taught in the card."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which documented history and new findings most strongly support immediate cefazolin hypersensitivity rather than a local infusion reaction?","point":"Recognize a relevant allergy history and a systemic immediate reaction.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prior nausea after amoxicillin, then tenderness limited to the cannulated vein with stable vital signs","explanation":"Nausea alone does not establish beta-lactam allergy, and the new signs suggest a local reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Prior immediate hives after cefazolin, then wheeze, facial swelling and hypotension within minutes of exposure","explanation":"This is consistent with possible anaphylaxis. Stop the exposure and activate the emergency response per protocol."},{"id":"C","text":"Prior nonspecific rash in childhood, then new watery diarrhea after several days of treatment","explanation":"Diarrhea during treatment requires evaluation, but the pattern is not that of immediate anaphylaxis."},{"id":"D","text":"Prior cefazolin tolerance, then focal IV-site swelling without wheeze or vital sign changes","explanation":"Focal site changes require review but do not match the immediate systemic pattern."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"While a patient receives repeated IV cefazolin for a susceptible infection, estimated creatinine clearance falls substantially. Which change should the nurse anticipate reviewing with the prescribing team?","point":"Apply reduced renal clearance to the maintenance dose and interval.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether the maintenance dose needs to increase because slower clearance reduces exposure","explanation":"Reduced clearance may increase exposure, not reduce it."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether the maintenance regimen can continue unchanged because liver enzymes are unchanged","explanation":"Stable liver enzymes do not establish adequate renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether only IV dilution needs adjustment while exposure remains unchanged","explanation":"Dilution does not address reduced renal elimination or the systemic dose schedule."},{"id":"D","text":"Whether the maintenance dose or interval needs adjustment to prevent accumulation","explanation":"Cefazolin is predominantly renally cleared. Apply the appropriate product and indication-specific regimen."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which new course after several days of cefazolin most strongly supports escalation for possible antibiotic-associated colitis?","point":"Recognize persistent watery diarrhea with inflammatory features during antibiotic exposure.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"One soft stool after a prescribed laxative with no recurrence","explanation":"A single transient stool change is less suggestive than persistent watery diarrhea."},{"id":"B","text":"Repeated watery stools with new lower abdominal cramping","explanation":"This pattern warrants assessment for C. difficile and other causes of antibiotic-associated diarrhea."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stools and cramping relieved by defecation","explanation":"This pattern suggests constipation rather than typical antibiotic-associated diarrhea."},{"id":"D","text":"Intermittent nausea around meals without a stool change","explanation":"Nausea may occur without colitis. It is not the bowel pattern asked for."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The operating room moves a case earlier. Which event in the revised schedule should determine when the cefazolin prophylaxis dose is given?","point":"Anchor prophylaxis to the anticipated incision time.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The revised time of anaesthesia assessment","explanation":"Anaesthesia assessment may move independently of incision and does not replace the incision timing reference."},{"id":"B","text":"The revised time of patient transfer to the operating room","explanation":"Transfer is a workflow step and does not establish the interval before incision."},{"id":"C","text":"The revised time of surgical incision","explanation":"Coordinate timing with the surgical protocol so protective exposure is present at incision."},{"id":"D","text":"The revised time of postoperative handover","explanation":"This falls after the operative contamination risk that prophylaxis addresses."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient treated with cefazolin for an established infection reports less pain after analgesia, but fever persists and the microbiology report is now available. Which pair most directly supports reassessing the cefazolin plan?","point":"Use clinical infection response and culture susceptibility together.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The trend in pain scores and the isolate’s susceptibility to a different drug only","explanation":"This does not assess cefazolin susceptibility or the clinical infection trend."},{"id":"B","text":"The trend in bowel frequency and the time of the last cefazolin dose","explanation":"These help evaluate tolerance and administration rather than microbiological suitability."},{"id":"C","text":"The creatinine trend and the dilution used for the last dose","explanation":"These inform safety and preparation rather than clinical infection response and susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"The trend in infection findings and the isolate’s susceptibility to cefazolin","explanation":"Less pain alone is not an antibacterial endpoint. Susceptibility and clinical response inform regimen review."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antimicrobial_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"During IV cefazolin, the cannulated vein becomes warm, red and tender along its course. Which local pattern best fits?","point":"Distinguish phlebitis from infiltration and mechanical obstruction.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Infiltration marked mainly by cool, diffuse swelling around the cannula","explanation":"This is not the characteristic pattern described."},{"id":"B","text":"Positional flow obstruction without venous inflammation","explanation":"A mechanical obstruction alone does not explain warmth, pain and linear redness."},{"id":"C","text":"Infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"Warmth, linear erythema and tenderness along a cannulated vein support phlebitis and require site assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Insertion-site bruising without vein tenderness","explanation":"Bruising does not account for the inflammatory pattern tracking along the vein."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--iv_site","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::iv_site"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which bacterial binding target explains cefazolin’s action as a beta-lactam?","point":"Identify penicillin-binding proteins as the target.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dihydrofolate reductase","explanation":"This belongs to the folate pathway and is not the beta-lactam target."},{"id":"B","text":"DNA gyrase","explanation":"This is a fluoroquinolone target."},{"id":"C","text":"Penicillin-binding proteins","explanation":"Cefazolin binds PBPs, including those that catalyze cell-wall cross-linking."},{"id":"D","text":"The 30S ribosomal subunit","explanation":"This is a target of some protein-synthesis inhibitors, such as aminoglycosides."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--cefazolin_pbp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_pbp"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The chart specifies IV cefazolin infusion. Pharmacy replaces the previous cefazolin vial with a different presentation whose instructions specify a different final dilution. Which plan best follows the ordered administration?","point":"Apply the replacement product’s own dilution and infusion instructions.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the replacement product’s labeled dilution and infusion instructions for the ordered dose","explanation":"Preparation and administration requirements depend on the exact cefazolin product."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the old final dilution and only change the reconstitution step","explanation":"Product differences can extend to final dilution and administration as well as reconstitution."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the replacement dilution but administer as the IV bolus permitted for the previous product","explanation":"The order specifies infusion, and the previous product’s route instructions do not transfer."},{"id":"D","text":"Use the old product’s reconstitution and final dilution at the new product’s infusion rate","explanation":"The old preparation cannot automatically be substituted for the supplied presentation."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--cefazolin_product_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_product_preparation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pharmacologic goal explains why cefazolin is given before incision for surgical prophylaxis?","point":"Require effective antibiotic exposure in the tissue during the operative contamination window.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Allow tissue levels to peak only after closure to protect the postoperative wound","explanation":"Starting exposure after closure misses the operative risk period."},{"id":"B","text":"Obtain a pre-incision serum level even if tissue penetration occurs after incision","explanation":"The objective is effective tissue exposure during possible contamination."},{"id":"C","text":"Delay antibacterial activity until an operative culture identifies infection","explanation":"Prophylaxis aims to prevent infection and is given before an established infection is demonstrated."},{"id":"D","text":"Provide adequate antibacterial concentrations in tissue when contamination can first occur","explanation":"Cefazolin prophylaxis depends on tissue exposure at incision and during the relevant operative period."}],"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--comprehensive--cefazolin_tissue_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_tissue_timing"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]}],"questionCount":12,"correctionRevision":"cefazolin-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-cefazolin","title":"CEFAZOLIN INJECTION, POWDER, FOR SOLUTION [HENRY SCHEIN INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=922fb1a1-ad4c-4a12-8d5d-00fe13af18e8"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial.","mechanism":"Beta-lactam cephalosporin that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed.","administration":"Parenteral product; preparation and IV administration depend on the exact vial/product and order. Do not memorize one dilution or rate across all cefazolin products. Adjusted dosing may be needed with renal impairment.","monitor":"• Verify beta-lactam allergy history and watch for hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and ordered dose/interval. • Monitor IV site and clinical response. • Watch for significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea/C. difficile features. • For prophylaxis, timing relative to surgery matters—follow the surgical protocol/order.","why":"Cefazolin inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. In surgery, adequate tissue levels at the time of incision help reduce surgical-site infection risk.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Exact vial strength/formulation in the ward was not recorded; verify the vial, diluent, route and local order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial.","id":"section-0-f02fbe3a70853711"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Beta-lactam cephalosporin that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-5ff8fc6a43a3e42c"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed.","id":"section-2-c769a22cf41316d8"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Parenteral product; preparation and IV administration depend on the exact vial/product and order. Do not memorize one dilution or rate across all cefazolin products. Adjusted dosing may be needed with renal impairment.","id":"section-3-f71b769025b1696c"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Verify beta-lactam allergy history and watch for hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis. • Review renal function and ordered dose/interval. • Monitor IV site and clinical response. • Watch for significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea/C. difficile features. • For prophylaxis, timing relative to surgery matters—follow the surgical protocol/order.","id":"section-4-7293dc59d973d282"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Cefazolin inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis. In surgery, adequate tissue levels at the time of incision help reduce surgical-site infection risk.","id":"section-5-7ad5c601afcf2210"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026. Exact vial strength/formulation in the ward was not recorded; verify the vial, diluent, route and local order.","id":"section-6-28cbcb06fbfc8737"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"B","text":"Predominantly alpha1-mediated vasoconstriction","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"C","text":"Inhibits serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces GI/GU smooth-muscle spasm","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Cefazolin is a first-generation cephalosporin beta-lactam acting through PBPs."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does CEFAZOLIN belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Lincosamide","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Fluoroquinolone","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"C","text":"Polyene antifungal","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."},{"id":"D","text":"First-generation cephalosporin","explanation":"First-generation cephalosporin antibacterial."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relief of bronchospasm by beta2 stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Perioperative prophylaxis or susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Highly relevant to orthopaedics for perioperative antibacterial prophylaxis and for treatment of susceptible bacterial infections when prescribed."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Minutes after starting CEFAZOLIN, a patient develops wheeze, facial swelling, and hypotension. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"A local vein reaction does not explain the multisystem findings."},{"id":"B","text":"Delayed marrow suppression","explanation":"This does not fit the rapid onset or the described manifestations."},{"id":"C","text":"Anaphylaxis","explanation":"Acute airway and circulatory compromise after exposure is strongly concerning."},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not explain the immediate airway and circulatory pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"B","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"During CEFAZOLIN therapy, which new bowel pattern most warrants assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Normal formed stool after breakfast","explanation":"This is not a new concerning diarrhea pattern."},{"id":"B","text":"Frequent, persistent watery diarrhea","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea during antibiotic exposure needs assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced stool frequency during immobility","explanation":"This does not identify the complication being tested."},{"id":"D","text":"Hard stool with straining","explanation":"This points toward constipation rather than colitis-related diarrhea."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"When CEFAZOLIN is ordered for perioperative prophylaxis, which event determines the key timing relationship?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The surgical incision","explanation":"Protective antibacterial exposure is needed during operative contamination risk."},{"id":"B","text":"The first postoperative fever","explanation":"Waiting for fever would not meet a prophylactic purpose."},{"id":"C","text":"The first postoperative dressing change","explanation":"The protective aim precedes this later event."},{"id":"D","text":"The first postoperative meal","explanation":"Resumption of food is not the principal prophylaxis timing target."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Perioperative antibiotic timing should provide tissue exposure at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A bacterial infection is being treated with CEFAZOLIN. Which paired findings most directly inform whether the antimicrobial plan remains suitable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Appetite and bowel frequency","explanation":"These inform general tolerance but not the organism-directed treatment decision."},{"id":"B","text":"Infection response and culture susceptibility","explanation":"Clinical progress and organism susceptibility inform appropriateness."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain response and analgesic requirement","explanation":"These can improve without establishing antimicrobial susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"Fluid balance and serum sodium","explanation":"These inform hydration but do not establish antimicrobial appropriateness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"During IV CEFAZOLIN, warmth, redness and tenderness develop along the cannulated vein. Which local complication best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Local bruising after cannula insertion","explanation":"Bruising does not best explain warmth and tenderness tracking along the vein."},{"id":"B","text":"Mechanical catheter occlusion without inflammation","explanation":"Occlusion alone does not explain the inflammatory signs along the vein."},{"id":"C","text":"Infusion-site irritation or phlebitis","explanation":"Pain and erythema along a cannulated vein need local assessment."},{"id":"D","text":"Infiltration into the surrounding tissue","explanation":"The warm, tender vein pattern favors phlebitis over the typical cool, swollen infiltration site."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Inspect an IV site for treatment-related irritation or phlebitis.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--iv_site","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::iv_site"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which bacterial target does cefazolin bind?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bacterial dihydrofolate reductase","explanation":"This is a folate-pathway target, not cefazolin’s target."},{"id":"B","text":"Penicillin-binding proteins","explanation":"Binding these proteins inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis."},{"id":"C","text":"Bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit","explanation":"This is a protein-synthesis target, not cefazolin’s target."},{"id":"D","text":"Bacterial DNA gyrase","explanation":"This is associated with fluoroquinolone action, not cefazolin’s target."}],"point":"Identify penicillin-binding proteins as cefazolin’s taught bacterial target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--standard--cefazolin_pbp","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_pbp"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":10},{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Before CEFAZOLIN, which prior antibiotic reaction carries the strongest immediate hypersensitivity concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Local IV discomfort without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less specific for a serious immediate systemic allergy."},{"id":"B","text":"Metallic taste during metronidazole therapy","explanation":"This is a different, commonly nonallergic adverse effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Rapid wheeze and collapse after a beta-lactam","explanation":"The timing and cardiorespiratory involvement suggest a serious immediate reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Diarrhea several days into clindamycin therapy","explanation":"This needs review but is not the same immediate reaction pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Renal clearance falls during CEFAZOLIN therapy while the dose and interval remain unchanged. Which pharmacokinetic change is most concerning?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A shorter elimination half-life","explanation":"Reduced clearance tends to prolong, not shorten, elimination."},{"id":"B","text":"Increased drug accumulation","explanation":"Reduced renal elimination can raise exposure between doses."},{"id":"C","text":"Decreased drug accumulation","explanation":"This is the opposite of the expected effect of reduced clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"A lower steady-state exposure","explanation":"With unchanged dosing, reduced clearance can increase exposure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient develops persistent watery diarrhea after CEFAZOLIN. Which explanation should be considered during assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An immediate IgE-mediated airway reaction","explanation":"The case describes a gastrointestinal pattern rather than acute anaphylaxis."},{"id":"B","text":"An intended increase in osmotic laxative activity","explanation":"The antibiotic is not being used as an osmotic laxative."},{"id":"C","text":"Therapeutic stimulation of intestinal glucose absorption","explanation":"This does not explain the antibiotic-associated diarrhea warning."},{"id":"D","text":"Antibiotic-associated disturbance of intestinal flora","explanation":"This can permit C. difficile-associated disease."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Surgery is delayed after CEFAZOLIN prophylaxis was scheduled. Which relationship needs review under the surgical protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose timing relative to the next pain score","explanation":"Analgesic assessment does not determine antibacterial exposure at incision."},{"id":"B","text":"Dose timing relative to the next bowel movement","explanation":"Bowel timing does not establish prophylactic surgical exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Dose timing relative to the first mobilization attempt","explanation":"Mobilization is not the defining perioperative prophylaxis timing target."},{"id":"D","text":"Dose timing relative to the revised incision time","explanation":"The delay may change whether exposure matches the operative risk period."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Perioperative antibiotic timing should provide tissue exposure at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--prophylaxis_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::prophylaxis_timing"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Two cefazolin products are supplied with different preparation instructions. What determines which dilution and IV method to use?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The dilution used for the last cefazolin patient","explanation":"A previous patient’s product and order may differ."},{"id":"B","text":"The supplied product instructions and the authorized order","explanation":"Preparation and IV administration are product- and order-specific."},{"id":"C","text":"The method used for any cefazolin vial of that strength","explanation":"Strength alone does not establish identical preparation or administration instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"The method used for another cephalosporin at the same dose","explanation":"Related antibiotics are not automatically interchangeable in preparation."}],"point":"Verify the exact cefazolin product and order before selecting preparation and IV administration instructions.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--cefazolin_product_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_product_preparation"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Why does cefazolin prophylaxis need to be related to incision time?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"To provide adequate tissue antibiotic levels when contamination may occur","explanation":"The rationale is adequate tissue exposure at incision."},{"id":"B","text":"To prevent the kidney from eliminating any antibiotic during surgery","explanation":"Elimination continues; it is not the reason for incision-related timing."},{"id":"C","text":"To produce the highest antibiotic level only after wound closure","explanation":"Waiting until closure misses the purpose of exposure at incision."},{"id":"D","text":"To ensure antibiotic activity begins only once infection is confirmed","explanation":"Prophylaxis precedes established infection rather than waiting for confirmation."}],"point":"Explain prophylaxis timing through adequate tissue antibiotic levels at incision.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--advanced--cefazolin_tissue_timing","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-cefazolin::cefazolin_tissue_timing"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":6},{"id":"ibd2-cefazolin--7-day","itemId":"ibd2-cefazolin","itemType":"medication","title":"CEFAZOLIN","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"comprehensive memory retest; questions may repeat","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of CEFAZOLIN?","choices":[