al tramadol capsule ordered for a fracture patient is temporarily unavailable. Stock includes a parenteral tramadol product of the same nominal milligram strength. Which principle applies?","point":"Distinguish the oral capsule from a parenteral formulation and reject an unverified conversion.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the oral order with the matching capsule or obtain a verified replacement regimen","explanation":"The same milligrams do not establish interchangeability between oral and parenteral products."},{"id":"B","text":"Inject the parenteral product on the oral schedule because the labeled milligrams match","explanation":"A change of route calls for an authorized and verified regimen."},{"id":"C","text":"Give the parenteral product orally using the capsule directions","explanation":"A parenteral formulation is not automatically appropriate for oral administration."},{"id":"D","text":"Change routes and copy the dosing interval from another patient","explanation":"Another order does not establish the route conversion or dose for this patient."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::formulation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"tramadol-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ibd2-tramadol--21-day","itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","itemType":"medication","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the direction of tramadol’s direct effect at the mu-opioid receptor?","point":"Identify weak agonism, distinct from antagonism or indirect effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Competitive antagonism","explanation":"This would inhibit rather than contribute to opioid analgesia."},{"id":"B","text":"Irreversible antagonism","explanation":"Tramadol does not block the mu receptor in this way."},{"id":"C","text":"Weak agonism","explanation":"Tramadol has weak mu-opioid agonism, and its active metabolite contributes to opioid analgesia."},{"id":"D","text":"No mu activity, with opioid effects limited to kappa antagonism","explanation":"The analgesic mechanism includes mu activity."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which therapeutic grouping determines how oral tramadol should be treated in an analgesic safety review?","point":"Classify tramadol as an opioid with additional monoamine effects.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Opioid analgesic with monoamine reuptake effects","explanation":"The additional mechanism does not remove the opioid classification or safety risks."},{"id":"B","text":"Non-opioid monoamine analgesic","explanation":"Tramadol has clinically important opioid activity as well as monoamine effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Non-opioid anti-inflammatory analgesic","explanation":"Tramadol does not act primarily as an NSAID."},{"id":"D","text":"Opioid antagonist analgesic","explanation":"Tramadol provides opioid analgesia through agonist activity."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After fracture fixation, ordered non-opioid measures do not adequately control moderate-to-severe acute pain. The prescriber selects oral tramadol. Which role is intended?","point":"Recognize prescribed opioid analgesia when other pain measures are inadequate.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treat fracture swelling as the primary anti-inflammatory component","explanation":"Tramadol is an analgesic rather than an anti-inflammatory drug."},{"id":"B","text":"Replace operative local anaesthesia through direct nerve blockade","explanation":"Its oral opioid and reuptake effects do not provide local nerve blockade."},{"id":"C","text":"Treat the acute pain as a prescribed opioid component","explanation":"Use is guided by pain severity, adequacy of alternatives and the individual order."},{"id":"D","text":"Treat muscle spasm as the primary skeletal muscle relaxant","explanation":"Tramadol can change pain perception but is not a skeletal muscle relaxant."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After tramadol, a postoperative patient is hard to wake and has slow, shallow breaths. Which interpretation should guide escalation even if oxygen saturation is preserved on supplemental oxygen?","point":"Detect impaired ventilation and reduced responsiveness despite apparently adequate saturations.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Possible opioid-induced ventilatory depression requiring immediate assessment","explanation":"Supplemental oxygen can preserve SpO2 even while ventilation is inadequate. Escalate and support airway and breathing per protocol."},{"id":"B","text":"Expected sedation with adequate ventilation, as shown by preserved SpO2","explanation":"SpO2 does not establish the adequacy of ventilation or consciousness."},{"id":"C","text":"Likely serotonin toxicity based on reduced respiratory drive alone","explanation":"Serotonin toxicity typically includes neuromuscular excitation and autonomic signs."},{"id":"D","text":"Likely orthostatic intolerance even while supine and hypoventilating","explanation":"This does not account for difficult arousal and depressed respiratory effort."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--opioid_respiration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::opioid_respiration"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After tramadol is added to a regimen containing an SSRI, an adult has agitation, diaphoresis, tremor and inducible clonus. Which interpretation best fits?","point":"Distinguish serotonergic neuromuscular excitation from competing toxidromes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression","explanation":"This more often presents with reduced arousal and ventilatory drive rather than clonus and agitation."},{"id":"B","text":"Anticholinergic toxicity from the opioid component","explanation":"Clonus and sweating are not the typical dry anticholinergic pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Serotonin toxicity from additive serotonergic effects","explanation":"Clonus and tremor with autonomic changes fit serotonin toxicity. Escalate promptly."},{"id":"D","text":"Uncomplicated postoperative pain with an adrenergic response","explanation":"Pain can cause sweating and agitation but does not explain the new clonus."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--serotonin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::serotonin"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history specifically increases concern about tramadol-associated convulsions even when it is given as prescribed?","point":"Identify a seizure-risk factor and recognize that risk is not limited to overdose.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Remote uncomplicated peptic ulcer disease","explanation":"This matters for NSAID selection but does not in itself identify tramadol-associated seizure risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Epilepsy or a history of seizures","explanation":"Tramadol can provoke seizures at recommended doses. Prior seizures increase concern and warrant review."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable chronic constipation with no seizure history","explanation":"Constipation needs separate management but does not identify this neurologic susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"A prior episode of opioid-related nausea without convulsions","explanation":"This informs tolerability rather than specifically increasing seizure susceptibility."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::seizure_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient due for a first assisted mobilization becomes dizzy and more drowsy after oral tramadol, with breathing unchanged. Which interpretation best fits the drug effect?","point":"Link dizziness and sedative impairment to fall risk even without respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The stable respiratory rate excludes medication-related impairment of mobility","explanation":"Fall risk can rise without measurable respiratory depression."},{"id":"B","text":"Tramadol-related dizziness and sedation can impair safe transfer even with stable breathing","explanation":"Reassess alertness, balance and mobility assistance before the transfer."},{"id":"C","text":"The new symptoms indicate adequate analgesia for independent transfer","explanation":"Analgesic benefit does not establish safe coordination or alertness."},{"id":"D","text":"The symptoms establish serotonin toxicity without a neuromuscular or autonomic change","explanation":"Dizziness and drowsiness alone are more consistent with sedation than a serotonergic syndrome."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::sedation_falls"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which bowel trend is most consistent with an opioid effect after several days of tramadol in an immobilized patient?","point":"Recognize slowed transit as a common opioid effect.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduced stool frequency with hard stools and straining","explanation":"Opioids can slow intestinal transit, and immobility may contribute."},{"id":"B","text":"New frequent watery stools after a course of antibiotics","explanation":"This pattern suggests diarrhea and warrants a separate assessment."},{"id":"C","text":"Black tarry stool with new postural symptoms","explanation":"This suggests possible bleeding rather than an uncomplicated opioid bowel effect."},{"id":"D","text":"Frequent loose stools with cramping after a laxative dose","explanation":"This is less characteristic of opioid-related slowing."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::constipation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Pain on transfer was 7/10 before tramadol. Which follow-up documents analgesic benefit and separately checks a key opioid hazard?","point":"Pair comparable movement pain with sedation and respiratory surveillance.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rest pain is 4/10, and a normal BP is used to infer safe ventilation","explanation":"Rest and movement pain are not directly comparable, and BP does not establish ventilation."},{"id":"B","text":"Transfer pain stays at 7/10, but drowsiness is used as evidence of pain relief","explanation":"Sedation does not establish analgesic benefit."},{"id":"C","text":"No transfer is attempted, and lower stool frequency is treated as evidence of analgesia","explanation":"Bowel slowing can be an adverse effect, not an indicator of pain relief."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain on the same transfer falls to 4/10, with respiratory effort and alertness reassessed","explanation":"A comparable activity measures analgesia. Separate checks assess opioid-related harm."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::pain_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which neurotransmitter reuptake pair contributes to tramadol’s analgesic and interaction profile?","point":"Identify serotonin and norepinephrine together.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Serotonin and dopamine","explanation":"Serotonin fits but dopamine is not the other named pathway."},{"id":"B","text":"Serotonin and norepinephrine","explanation":"Both reuptake pathways are involved."},{"id":"C","text":"Norepinephrine and dopamine","explanation":"Norepinephrine fits but dopamine is not the other relevant pathway."},{"id":"D","text":"Norepinephrine and acetylcholine","explanation":"Acetylcholine is not the other reuptake target."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--tramadol_monoamines","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_monoamines"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which two co-medications each increase concern about respiratory depression when added to tramadol?","point":"Recognize gabapentin and sedating promethazine as additive CNS depressants.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Gabapentin and paracetamol","explanation":"Gabapentin raises the concern. Paracetamol does not ordinarily add this CNS depressant effect."},{"id":"B","text":"Promethazine and ibuprofen","explanation":"Promethazine raises the concern. Ibuprofen does not ordinarily add CNS depressant activity."},{"id":"C","text":"Paracetamol and ibuprofen","explanation":"These non-opioids do not produce the named additive CNS depressant effect at usual doses."},{"id":"D","text":"Gabapentin and promethazine","explanation":"Both can enhance sedation when combined with an opioid and increase respiratory risk."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--tramadol_named_sedatives","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_named_sedatives"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Soon after the first oral tramadol dose, a patient reports nausea and mild dizziness without clonus, abnormal ventilation, or rash. What interpretation best fits this pattern?","point":"Recognize common tramadol adverse effects and distinguish them from severe syndromes.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Established serotonin toxicity based on gastrointestinal symptoms alone","explanation":"Serotonin toxicity requires assessment of additional characteristic features."},{"id":"B","text":"Common early adverse effects that still warrant symptom and safety reassessment","explanation":"Nausea and dizziness can occur without indicating a serious toxidrome."},{"id":"C","text":"Established opioid ventilatory depression without breathing or alertness changes","explanation":"Nausea alone does not establish compromised ventilation."},{"id":"D","text":"Established immediate hypersensitivity without skin, airway, or circulatory findings","explanation":"The findings are more compatible with known nonallergic adverse effects."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--tramadol_nausea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_nausea"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient has worsening renal function and chronic hepatic impairment while receiving oral tramadol. Which issue should the nurse raise before the next scheduled dose?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether declining clearance requires an earlier repeat dose to maintain a stable level","explanation":"Slower clearance can increase exposure rather than requiring more frequent dosing."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether a therapeutic pain response rules out accumulation risk","explanation":"Symptom relief does not establish that repeated exposure is safe."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether switching to parenteral tramadol removes the need for impairment review","explanation":"Changing route does not remove systemic metabolism or elimination concerns."},{"id":"D","text":"Whether accumulation warrants a lower dose, longer interval or a different analgesic plan","explanation":"Renal and hepatic impairment can affect tramadol and its active metabolite, and interactions can further change exposure."}],"point":"Recognize accumulation risk and need for formulation- and patient-specific dose or interval review.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--tramadol_organ_review","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::tramadol_organ_review"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The oral tramadol capsule ordered for a fracture patient is temporarily unavailable. Stock includes a parenteral tramadol product of the same nominal milligram strength. Which principle applies?","point":"Distinguish the oral capsule from a parenteral formulation and reject an unverified conversion.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Use the oral order with the matching capsule or obtain a verified replacement regimen","explanation":"The same milligrams do not establish interchangeability between oral and parenteral products."},{"id":"B","text":"Inject the parenteral product on the oral schedule because the labeled milligrams match","explanation":"A change of route calls for an authorized and verified regimen."},{"id":"C","text":"Give the parenteral product orally using the capsule directions","explanation":"A parenteral formulation is not automatically appropriate for oral administration."},{"id":"D","text":"Change routes and copy the dosing interval from another patient","explanation":"Another order does not establish the route conversion or dose for this patient."}],"id":"ibd2-tramadol--comprehensive--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::formulation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"tramadol-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-tramadol","title":"TRAMADOL HYDROCHLORIDE TABLET, EXTENDED RELEASE [PREFERRED PHARMACEUTICALS, INC.]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=83367367-5654-4078-831a-69ade48bc795"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects.","mechanism":"Weak mu-opioid receptor agonism plus inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.","use":"Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery.","administration":"For the oral capsule, give PO exactly as ordered and confirm formulation/strength. Do not assume oral and parenteral products are interchangeable. Avoid unverified route conversion. Dose selection can require adjustment for renal/hepatic impairment and interacting medicines.","monitor":"• Assess pain, respiratory rate/effort, sedation/level of consciousness and fall risk. • Serious respiratory depression can occur, especially with other CNS depressants. • Tramadol can cause seizures and serotonin syndrome. • Watch for nausea, dizziness and constipation. • Combining with gabapentin or sedating promethazine can increase sedation/respiratory risk. • Escalate excessive sedation, slow/shallow breathing or reduced responsiveness.","why":"Opioid activity provides analgesia, while monoamine effects contribute to both analgesia and interaction risks such as serotonin syndrome and seizures.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026, including an oral capsule. Other ward formulations may differ; verify the exact product, strength and route before administration."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects.","id":"section-0-e8332608edc23353"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Weak mu-opioid receptor agonism plus inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.","id":"section-1-346c86f53770937b"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery.","id":"section-2-4e1d69f5e663d5d7"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"For the oral capsule, give PO exactly as ordered and confirm formulation/strength. Do not assume oral and parenteral products are interchangeable. Avoid unverified route conversion. Dose selection can require adjustment for renal/hepatic impairment and interacting medicines.","id":"section-3-13b0acd2fd96e776"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Assess pain, respiratory rate/effort, sedation/level of consciousness and fall risk. • Serious respiratory depression can occur, especially with other CNS depressants. • Tramadol can cause seizures and serotonin syndrome. • Watch for nausea, dizziness and constipation. • Combining with gabapentin or sedating promethazine can increase sedation/respiratory risk. • Escalate excessive sedation, slow/shallow breathing or reduced responsiveness.","id":"section-4-5ca01be119c163f7"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Opioid activity provides analgesia, while monoamine effects contribute to both analgesia and interaction risks such as serotonin syndrome and seizures.","id":"section-5-2a38b5def33cade3"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 1 Oct 2026, including an oral capsule. Other ward formulations may differ; verify the exact product, strength and route before administration.","id":"section-6-b070a61de672e856"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard","itemId":"ibd2-tramadol","itemType":"medication","title":"TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Reduces angiotensin-II formation","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Retains water in the colon","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Predominantly beta1-mediated increased contractility","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Mu agonism and reuptake inhibition","explanation":"Tramadol combines weak mu activity with serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Non-opioid analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"B","text":"Pyrazolone analgesic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"C","text":"NSAID","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Opioid/monoamine analgesic","explanation":"Opioid analgesic with additional serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake effects."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::drug-class"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement therapy for an adrenal crisis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Prescribed treatment of moderate-to-severe pain","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Moderate-to-severe pain when an opioid analgesic is prescribed; common relevance after fractures, major trauma or surgery."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"After TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation, which change requires the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New nausea with preserved alertness","explanation":"This is less immediately threatening than reduced responsiveness with abnormal breathing."},{"id":"B","text":"New constipation and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These matter but do not outrank the described ventilatory threat."},{"id":"C","text":"New reduced responsiveness and shallow breathing","explanation":"This suggests a threat to ventilation from opioid toxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"New itching without airway or BP change","explanation":"This requires assessment but is less urgent than ventilation impairment."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize opioid-related sedation and respiratory depression.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--opioid_respiration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::opioid_respiration"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"After TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation, which syndrome best explains agitation, fever, hyperreflexia, and autonomic instability?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Opioid respiratory toxicity","explanation":"This more typically produces depressed breathing and consciousness."},{"id":"B","text":"Isolated antimuscarinic intolerance","explanation":"This does not best explain the hyperreflexic serotonergic pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Serotonin toxicity","explanation":"The combination of neuromuscular excitation and autonomic changes fits."},{"id":"D","text":"Vancomycin infusion intolerance","explanation":"This is a different administration-related reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize serotonin toxicity and review serotonergic combinations.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--serotonin","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::serotonin"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which new neurological event during TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation therapy needs the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An unchanged chronic headache","explanation":"The case does not describe a new neurological deterioration in this option."},{"id":"B","text":"A new generalized seizure","explanation":"This is a potentially serious neurological adverse effect or another emergency."},{"id":"C","text":"Improving original symptoms","explanation":"Therapeutic improvement does not itself identify neurotoxicity."},{"id":"D","text":"Mild unchanged nausea","explanation":"This is less urgent than a new seizure."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A patient taking TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation is newly drowsy and ataxic but has stable vital signs. Which activity most directly requires additional assistance?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Standing and transferring to the bathroom","explanation":"Impaired alertness and coordination increase fall risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Using the call bell while seated with support","explanation":"This does not require the impaired balance involved in standing."},{"id":"C","text":"Choosing a meal while seated with support","explanation":"This does not carry the same immediate fall exposure."},{"id":"D","text":"Reporting pain while seated with support","explanation":"The described impairment most directly compromises unsupported mobility."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Sedation, dizziness, and ataxia can increase fall risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--sedation_falls","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::sedation_falls"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"An immobilized patient receiving TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation develops reduced stool frequency and straining. Which problem best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Constipation","explanation":"The symptoms fit impaired bowel transit associated with opioid exposure and immobility."},{"id":"B","text":"Fecal incontinence","explanation":"The case describes difficult passage rather than involuntary stool loss."},{"id":"C","text":"Diarrhea","explanation":"Reduced stool frequency and straining do not describe diarrhea."},{"id":"D","text":"Upper gastrointestinal bleeding","explanation":"No bleeding finding is described."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Opioids can contribute to constipation and require bowel-pattern review.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-tramadol--standard--constipation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-tramadol::constipation"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to TRAMADOL — oral capsule / ward formulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"B","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."},{"id":"C","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."},{"id":"D","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"