:"Penicillin-binding proteins, impairing cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Meropenem binds bacterial PBPs and disrupts peptidoglycan synthesis."},{"id":"C","text":"The 30S ribosomal subunit, impairing bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is the mechanism of aminoglycosides rather than meropenem."},{"id":"D","text":"DNA gyrase, impairing DNA supercoiling","explanation":"That is a fluoroquinolone target."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which specific beta-lactam subgroup contains meropenem?","point":"Classify meropenem as a carbapenem.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Cephalosporins","explanation":"Cephalosporins are a distinct beta-lactam subgroup."},{"id":"B","text":"Penicillins","explanation":"Meropenem shares the beta-lactam ring but is not a penicillin."},{"id":"C","text":"Carbapenems","explanation":"Meropenem belongs to this broad-spectrum beta-lactam subgroup."},{"id":"D","text":"Monobactams","explanation":"Aztreonam is an example of this separate subgroup."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which adult orthopaedic ward scenario fits the labeled treatment role of meropenem?","point":"Select treatment of a serious susceptible complicated skin or soft-tissue infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A localized superficial fungal infection with no bacterial evidence","explanation":"Meropenem is an antibacterial, and the scenario does not establish a susceptible bacterial infection."},{"id":"B","text":"Routine pre-incision prophylaxis for uncomplicated elective orthopaedic surgery","explanation":"Selection of prophylaxis depends on the procedure and protocol. Broad spectrum alone does not justify meropenem."},{"id":"C","text":"Persistent inflammation after a closed injury without evidence of infection","explanation":"Meropenem does not treat noninfectious inflammation."},{"id":"D","text":"A documented susceptible organism in a serious complicated skin and soft-tissue infection","explanation":"Meropenem can treat selected complicated skin or skin-structure infections caused by susceptible organisms."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history and new response most strongly support immediate hypersensitivity to meropenem?","point":"Distinguish a relevant prior reaction and an immediate systemic reaction from nonallergic effects.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prior nausea with oral amoxicillin, then isolated burning at the cannula with stable vital signs","explanation":"Nausea alone does not establish beta-lactam allergy, and a local finding does not show a systemic reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"An undocumented childhood rash, then watery diarrhea after several days of meropenem","explanation":"The diarrhea requires assessment but does not fit immediate hypersensitivity."},{"id":"C","text":"Prior carbapenem tolerance, then cool swelling limited to the IV site with stable vital signs","explanation":"This suggests a local infusion problem rather than immediate systemic hypersensitivity."},{"id":"D","text":"Prior immediate hives after meropenem, then new wheeze and hypotension minutes into infusion","explanation":"This pattern raises concern for anaphylaxis. Stop exposure and activate the emergency response per protocol."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receives repeated IV meropenem. Estimated creatinine clearance declines substantially during treatment. Which part of the regimen should be reviewed?","point":"Apply renal clearance to adjustment of dose and interval.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The dose and interval because reduced clearance usually requires more frequent dosing","explanation":"Reduced clearance can lead to accumulation rather than requiring more frequent doses."},{"id":"B","text":"Only the infusion fluid because renal function changes preparation but not systemic exposure","explanation":"Preparation alone does not address reduced renal elimination."},{"id":"C","text":"The prescribed dose and interval because reduced clearance can increase exposure","explanation":"The regimen may require adjustment using the product and indication-specific instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"Only the hepatic panel because normal liver results establish adequate clearance","explanation":"Hepatic tests do not replace review of renal function for meropenem."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which new symptoms during meropenem treatment should prompt assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","point":"Recognize persistent watery stool and cramping as a concerning pattern.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeated watery stools with new abdominal cramping","explanation":"This pattern warrants assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"A single soft stool following a laxative, with no recurrence","explanation":"This is less suggestive than persistent watery diarrhea."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stools and straining with no fever","explanation":"This pattern is more consistent with constipation."},{"id":"D","text":"Nausea around an infusion with no bowel change","explanation":"Nausea alone does not identify the colitis pattern."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving meropenem develops acute renal impairment followed by new myoclonic jerks and confusion. Which possibility warrants prompt escalation?","point":"Recognize possible carbapenem neurotoxicity and seizure risk in renal impairment.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Meropenem accumulation may contribute to neurotoxicity and seizures","explanation":"Renal deterioration raises exposure risk. Assess urgently for neurotoxicity and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"The findings establish a local infusion reaction without central involvement","explanation":"A local reaction does not account for jerks and confusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced renal clearance makes meropenem neurotoxicity less likely","explanation":"Reduced clearance can increase exposure and risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Neurologic toxicity can be excluded if the patient did not have epilepsy before admission","explanation":"Seizures or other CNS adverse effects can occur without known epilepsy, particularly with renal impairment."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A serious infection improves after initial meropenem. The isolate is also susceptible to a narrower option appropriate for the site. Which issue should the team review?","point":"Apply culture-directed de-escalation alongside clinical response.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether to narrow treatment based on the isolate, infection site and clinical course","explanation":"Broad empiric therapy should be reassessed once reliable susceptibility data are available."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether improvement alone establishes that broad-spectrum therapy must continue","explanation":"Improvement does not remove the reason to consider an appropriate narrower agent."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether narrow therapy should be ruled out solely because meropenem was started first","explanation":"Initial empiric selection does not establish the best definitive regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Whether the narrower antibiotic can replace current therapy solely on the basis of disk susceptibility","explanation":"Susceptibility must be interpreted with infection site, clinical response and the prescribed plan."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antimicrobial_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The prescription is for an IV meropenem infusion. Pharmacy supplies a replacement presentation whose reconstitution and final dilution instructions differ. Which preparation matches the order?","point":"Apply the exact supplied product’s IV preparation and infusion requirements.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Keep the old reconstitution volume and use the new final dilution","explanation":"A change in presentation can change the initial reconstitution volume as well as final dilution."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the new reconstitution instructions but switch to the bolus method permitted for the old vial","explanation":"The IV infusion order and the supplied product determine the delivery method."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the replacement product’s reconstitution, dilution and infusion instructions for the verified order","explanation":"Product-specific preparation requirements must be matched to the authorized IV regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Keep the old dilution and rate if the replacement contains the same drug name","explanation":"Matching active ingredients does not establish matching preparation instructions."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--meropenem_iv_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_iv_preparation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient’s seizure control was stable on divalproex before meropenem. Which paired change best fits the known interaction?","point":"Recall the direction of the valproate change and the clinical consequence.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A marked rise in valproate concentration with dose-related sedation","explanation":"The characteristic carbapenem interaction lowers valproate concentration."},{"id":"B","text":"A marked fall in valproate concentration with breakthrough seizures","explanation":"Carbapenems can markedly reduce valproate exposure. Escalate to the prescribing team for a treatment plan."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable valproate concentrations with reduced meropenem exposure","explanation":"This misses the interaction involving lowered valproate."},{"id":"D","text":"A rise in valproate concentration with improved seizure control","explanation":"The direction and clinical effect are opposite to the known risk."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--valproate","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::valproate"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient is scheduled for an uncomplicated orthopaedic procedure. The ward has meropenem and the prophylactic agent specified by the local procedure protocol. Which approach is appropriate?","point":"Distinguish procedure-specific prophylaxis from broad treatment of a serious infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replace the indicated prophylactic agent with meropenem because it covers more organisms","explanation":"Broader activity does not automatically improve prevention and may increase unnecessary exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Add meropenem to the ordered prophylactic drug whenever a prosthesis is used","explanation":"This is not a routine indication for dual broad prophylaxis."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the meropenem regimen specified for a different patient with established complicated infection","explanation":"Treatment regimens for active infection do not determine routine prophylaxis."},{"id":"D","text":"Use the ordered procedure-specific prophylactic regimen unless a documented indication supports a different choice","explanation":"Broad activity and ward availability do not establish meropenem as routine prophylaxis."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--meropenem_not_prophylaxis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_not_prophylaxis"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with stable renal function is about to start meropenem. Which history still specifically raises concern about the drug’s seizure potential?","point":"Recognize pre-existing CNS disease despite preserved renal function.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A remote drug-related rash without neurologic involvement","explanation":"This may affect allergy assessment, rather than specifically increasing seizure vulnerability."},{"id":"B","text":"A prior seizure disorder requiring treatment","explanation":"CNS disease, including seizure history, can increase seizure risk despite preserved renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"A history of gastritis without neurologic disease","explanation":"This informs GI history but does not establish the CNS risk factor."},{"id":"D","text":"A prior episode of infusi