,"title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which target and downstream action match meropenem?","point":"Distinguish PBP binding and interruption of peptidoglycan synthesis.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Penicillin-binding proteins, impairing ribosomal translation","explanation":"The target is correct, but the affected pathway is the cell wall."},{"id":"B","text":"Penicillin-binding proteins, impairing cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Meropenem binds bacterial PBPs and disrupts peptidoglycan synthesis."},{"id":"C","text":"The 30S ribosomal subunit, impairing bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is the mechanism of aminoglycosides rather than meropenem."},{"id":"D","text":"DNA gyrase, impairing DNA supercoiling","explanation":"That is a fluoroquinolone target."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which specific beta-lactam subgroup contains meropenem?","point":"Classify meropenem as a carbapenem.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Cephalosporins","explanation":"Cephalosporins are a distinct beta-lactam subgroup."},{"id":"B","text":"Penicillins","explanation":"Meropenem shares the beta-lactam ring but is not a penicillin."},{"id":"C","text":"Carbapenems","explanation":"Meropenem belongs to this broad-spectrum beta-lactam subgroup."},{"id":"D","text":"Monobactams","explanation":"Aztreonam is an example of this separate subgroup."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which adult orthopaedic ward scenario fits the labeled treatment role of meropenem?","point":"Select treatment of a serious susceptible complicated skin or soft-tissue infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A localized superficial fungal infection with no bacterial evidence","explanation":"Meropenem is an antibacterial, and the scenario does not establish a susceptible bacterial infection."},{"id":"B","text":"Routine pre-incision prophylaxis for uncomplicated elective orthopaedic surgery","explanation":"Selection of prophylaxis depends on the procedure and protocol. Broad spectrum alone does not justify meropenem."},{"id":"C","text":"Persistent inflammation after a closed injury without evidence of infection","explanation":"Meropenem does not treat noninfectious inflammation."},{"id":"D","text":"A documented susceptible organism in a serious complicated skin and soft-tissue infection","explanation":"Meropenem can treat selected complicated skin or skin-structure infections caused by susceptible organisms."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which history and new response most strongly support immediate hypersensitivity to meropenem?","point":"Distinguish a relevant prior reaction and an immediate systemic reaction from nonallergic effects.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Prior nausea with oral amoxicillin, then isolated burning at the cannula with stable vital signs","explanation":"Nausea alone does not establish beta-lactam allergy, and a local finding does not show a systemic reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"An undocumented childhood rash, then watery diarrhea after several days of meropenem","explanation":"The diarrhea requires assessment but does not fit immediate hypersensitivity."},{"id":"C","text":"Prior carbapenem tolerance, then cool swelling limited to the IV site with stable vital signs","explanation":"This suggests a local infusion problem rather than immediate systemic hypersensitivity."},{"id":"D","text":"Prior immediate hives after meropenem, then new wheeze and hypotension minutes into infusion","explanation":"This pattern raises concern for anaphylaxis. Stop exposure and activate the emergency response per protocol."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receives repeated IV meropenem. Estimated creatinine clearance declines substantially during treatment. Which part of the regimen should be reviewed?","point":"Apply renal clearance to adjustment of dose and interval.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The dose and interval because reduced clearance usually requires more frequent dosing","explanation":"Reduced clearance can lead to accumulation rather than requiring more frequent doses."},{"id":"B","text":"Only the infusion fluid because renal function changes preparation but not systemic exposure","explanation":"Preparation alone does not address reduced renal elimination."},{"id":"C","text":"The prescribed dose and interval because reduced clearance can increase exposure","explanation":"The regimen may require adjustment using the product and indication-specific instructions."},{"id":"D","text":"Only the hepatic panel because normal liver results establish adequate clearance","explanation":"Hepatic tests do not replace review of renal function for meropenem."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which new symptoms during meropenem treatment should prompt assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","point":"Recognize persistent watery stool and cramping as a concerning pattern.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repeated watery stools with new abdominal cramping","explanation":"This pattern warrants assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"A single soft stool following a laxative, with no recurrence","explanation":"This is less suggestive than persistent watery diarrhea."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stools and straining with no fever","explanation":"This pattern is more consistent with constipation."},{"id":"D","text":"Nausea around an infusion with no bowel change","explanation":"Nausea alone does not identify the colitis pattern."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient receiving meropenem develops acute renal impairment followed by new myoclonic jerks and confusion. Which possibility warrants prompt escalation?","point":"Recognize possible carbapenem neurotoxicity and seizure risk in renal impairment.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Meropenem accumulation may contribute to neurotoxicity and seizures","explanation":"Renal deterioration raises exposure risk. Assess urgently for neurotoxicity and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"The findings establish a local infusion reaction without central involvement","explanation":"A local reaction does not account for jerks and confusion."},{"id":"C","text":"Reduced renal clearance makes meropenem neurotoxicity less likely","explanation":"Reduced clearance can increase exposure and risk."},{"id":"D","text":"Neurologic toxicity can be excluded if the patient did not have epilepsy before admission","explanation":"Seizures or other CNS adverse effects can occur without known epilepsy, particularly with renal impairment."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A serious infection improves after initial meropenem. The isolate is also susceptible to a narrower option appropriate for the site. Which issue should the team review?","point":"Apply culture-directed de-escalation alongside clinical response.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Whether to narrow treatment based on the isolate, infection site and clinical course","explanation":"Broad empiric therapy should be reassessed once reliable susceptibility data are available."},{"id":"B","text":"Whether improvement alone establishes that broad-spectrum therapy must continue","explanation":"Improvement does not remove the reason to consider an appropriate narrower agent."},{"id":"C","text":"Whether narrow therapy should be ruled out solely because meropenem was started first","explanation":"Initial empiric selection does not establish the best definitive regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Whether the narrower antibiotic can replace current therapy solely on the basis of disk susceptibility","explanation":"Susceptibility must be interpreted with infection site, clinical response and the prescribed plan."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antimicrobial_response"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"The prescription is for an IV meropenem infusion. Pharmacy supplies a replacement presentation whose reconstitution and final dilution instructions differ. Which preparation matches the order?","point":"Apply the exact supplied product’s IV preparation and infusion requirements.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Keep the old reconstitution volume and use the new final dilution","explanation":"A change in presentation can change the initial reconstitution volume as well as final dilution."},{"id":"B","text":"Use the new reconstitution instructions but switch to the bolus method permitted for the old vial","explanation":"The IV infusion order and the supplied product determine the delivery method."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the replacement product’s reconstitution, dilution and infusion instructions for the verified order","explanation":"Product-specific preparation requirements must be matched to the authorized IV regimen."},{"id":"D","text":"Keep the old dilution and rate if the replacement contains the same drug name","explanation":"Matching active ingredients does not establish matching preparation instructions."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--meropenem_iv_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_iv_preparation"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient’s seizure control was stable on divalproex before meropenem. Which paired change best fits the known interaction?","point":"Recall the direction of the valproate change and the clinical consequence.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A marked rise in valproate concentration with dose-related sedation","explanation":"The characteristic carbapenem interaction lowers valproate concentration."},{"id":"B","text":"A marked fall in valproate concentration with breakthrough seizures","explanation":"Carbapenems can markedly reduce valproate exposure. Escalate to the prescribing team for a treatment plan."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable valproate concentrations with reduced meropenem exposure","explanation":"This misses the interaction involving lowered valproate."},{"id":"D","text":"A rise in valproate concentration with improved seizure control","explanation":"The direction and clinical effect are opposite to the known risk."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--valproate","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::valproate"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient is scheduled for an uncomplicated orthopaedic procedure. The ward has meropenem and the prophylactic agent specified by the local procedure protocol. Which approach is appropriate?","point":"Distinguish procedure-specific prophylaxis from broad treatment of a serious infection.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"D","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replace the indicated prophylactic agent with meropenem because it covers more organisms","explanation":"Broader activity does not automatically improve prevention and may increase unnecessary exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Add meropenem to the ordered prophylactic drug whenever a prosthesis is used","explanation":"This is not a routine indication for dual broad prophylaxis."},{"id":"C","text":"Use the meropenem regimen specified for a different patient with established complicated infection","explanation":"Treatment regimens for active infection do not determine routine prophylaxis."},{"id":"D","text":"Use the ordered procedure-specific prophylactic regimen unless a documented indication supports a different choice","explanation":"Broad activity and ward availability do not establish meropenem as routine prophylaxis."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--meropenem_not_prophylaxis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_not_prophylaxis"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with stable renal function is about to start meropenem. Which history still specifically raises concern about the drug’s seizure potential?","point":"Recognize pre-existing CNS disease despite preserved renal function.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A remote drug-related rash without neurologic involvement","explanation":"This may affect allergy assessment, rather than specifically increasing seizure vulnerability."},{"id":"B","text":"A prior seizure disorder requiring treatment","explanation":"CNS disease, including seizure history, can increase seizure risk despite preserved renal clearance."},{"id":"C","text":"A history of gastritis without neurologic disease","explanation":"This informs GI history but does not establish the CNS risk factor."},{"id":"D","text":"A prior episode of infusion-site irritation without neurologic symptoms","explanation":"This does not identify a predisposition to meropenem-associated seizures."}],"id":"ibd2-meropenem--comprehensive--meropenem_cns_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_cns_risk"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]}],"questionCount":12,"correctionRevision":"meropenem-source-review-20261116","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-meropenem","title":"MEROPENEM INJECTION, POWDER, FOR SOLUTION [WG CRITICAL CARE, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=74ff19b5-6156-4a27-affb-744b0ce1aeec"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial.","mechanism":"Carbapenem beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","use":"Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum.","administration":"IV. Reconstitution, dilution, infusion method and dose depend on the exact product, indication and renal function. Current labeling requires dose adjustment when renal function is reduced.","monitor":"• Check beta-lactam allergy history and monitor for hypersensitivity. • Review renal function because accumulation increases adverse-effect risk. • Watch for seizures/neurologic changes, particularly with renal impairment or CNS disease. • Monitor for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • IMPORTANT interaction: meropenem can markedly lower valproic acid/divalproex concentrations and trigger breakthrough seizures.","why":"Meropenem inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis and has broad activity, but broad coverage does not replace culture-guided therapy and antimicrobial stewardship.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial strength was not recorded; verify the vial and order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial.","id":"section-0-172212d9f00e3bc8"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Carbapenem beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-a54bcc99c1436284"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum.","id":"section-2-cf20ecce68f2e7c1"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV. Reconstitution, dilution, infusion method and dose depend on the exact product, indication and renal function. Current labeling requires dose adjustment when renal function is reduced.","id":"section-3-3f245b647a1e82a2"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check beta-lactam allergy history and monitor for hypersensitivity. • Review renal function because accumulation increases adverse-effect risk. • Watch for seizures/neurologic changes, particularly with renal impairment or CNS disease. • Monitor for significant diarrhea/C. difficile. • IMPORTANT interaction: meropenem can markedly lower valproic acid/divalproex concentrations and trigger breakthrough seizures.","id":"section-4-df161e73a41a1c1d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Meropenem inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis and has broad activity, but broad coverage does not replace culture-guided therapy and antimicrobial stewardship.","id":"section-5-db38ab732427baf1"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"WARD-OBSERVED repeatedly in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial strength was not recorded; verify the vial and order.","id":"section-6-fa56b9860ce40dee"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard","itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","itemType":"medication","title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Dose-dependent dopaminergic and adrenergic effects","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"B","text":"Restores vitamin-K-dependent clotting factor activity","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"C","text":"Antagonizes NMDA receptors","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."},{"id":"D","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"Meropenem is a carbapenem beta-lactam acting through PBPs."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does MEROPENEM belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Carbapenem beta-lactam","explanation":"Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Glycopeptide","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"C","text":"Aminoglycoside","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."},{"id":"D","text":"Beta-lactam","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Carbapenem (penem) broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibacterial."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::drug-class"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Suppression of ventricular ectopy by cardiac sodium-channel blockade","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm by beta2 stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Treatment of serious susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Serious susceptible bacterial infections. Current labeling includes complicated skin/skin-structure infection; it should not be treated as routine orthopaedic prophylaxis simply because it is broad spectrum."},{"id":"D","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Minutes after starting MEROPENEM, a patient develops wheeze, facial swelling, and hypotension. Which reaction best fits?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Delayed marrow suppression","explanation":"This does not fit the rapid onset or the described manifestations."},{"id":"B","text":"Antibiotic-associated colitis","explanation":"This does not explain the immediate airway and circulatory pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Isolated infusion-site phlebitis","explanation":"A local vein reaction does not explain the multisystem findings."},{"id":"D","text":"Anaphylaxis","explanation":"Acute airway and circulatory compromise after exposure is strongly concerning."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review serious beta-lactam allergy and recognize anaphylaxis.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--betalactam_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::betalactam_allergy"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which result best informs assessment of accumulation risk and possible regimen adjustment for MEROPENEM?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"TSH trend and free thyroxine","explanation":"These assess thyroid function rather than renal elimination."},{"id":"B","text":"Creatinine trend and estimated GFR","explanation":"These inform renal clearance and regimen review."},{"id":"C","text":"ALT trend and serum bilirubin","explanation":"These assess hepatic injury rather than directly estimate renal clearance."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and prothrombin time","explanation":"These assess a coagulation pathway rather than renal clearance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Renal impairment can require review of drug exposure and the prescribed regimen.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--renal_dose","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::renal_dose"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"During MEROPENEM therapy, which new bowel pattern most warrants assessment for antibiotic-associated colitis?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Frequent, persistent watery diarrhea","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea during antibiotic exposure needs assessment for C. difficile and other causes."},{"id":"B","text":"Reduced stool frequency during immobility","explanation":"This does not identify the complication being tested."},{"id":"C","text":"Hard stool with straining","explanation":"This points toward constipation rather than colitis-related diarrhea."},{"id":"D","text":"Normal formed stool after breakfast","explanation":"This is not a new concerning diarrhea pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Significant antibiotic-associated diarrhea requires evaluation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--antibiotic_diarrhea","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antibiotic_diarrhea"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new neurological event during MEROPENEM therapy needs the most urgent assessment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An unchanged chronic headache","explanation":"The case does not describe a new neurological deterioration in this option."},{"id":"B","text":"Mild unchanged nausea","explanation":"This is less urgent than a new seizure."},{"id":"C","text":"A new generalized seizure","explanation":"This is a potentially serious neurological adverse effect or another emergency."},{"id":"D","text":"Improving original symptoms","explanation":"Therapeutic improvement does not itself identify neurotoxicity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Some treatments or their accumulation can provoke seizures.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--seizure_risk","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::seizure_risk"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"A bacterial infection is being treated with MEROPENEM. Which paired findings most directly inform whether the antimicrobial plan remains suitable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Appetite and bowel frequency","explanation":"These inform general tolerance but not the organism-directed treatment decision."},{"id":"B","text":"Infection response and culture susceptibility","explanation":"Clinical progress and organism susceptibility inform appropriateness."},{"id":"C","text":"Pain response and analgesic requirement","explanation":"These can improve without establishing antimicrobial susceptibility."},{"id":"D","text":"Fluid balance and serum sodium","explanation":"These inform hydration but do not establish antimicrobial appropriateness."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Use clinical response, cultures, and the authorized antimicrobial plan.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--antimicrobial_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::antimicrobial_response"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which information should be verified before preparing and administering IV meropenem?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The usual broad-spectrum antibiotic dilution and adult schedule","explanation":"Broad-spectrum classification does not establish product-specific preparation or dosing."},{"id":"B","text":"The ward’s usual cefazolin method and the same milligram dose","explanation":"A different antibiotic’s preparation cannot establish the meropenem method."},{"id":"C","text":"The supplied product instructions and the renal-reviewed order","explanation":"These establish the appropriate preparation and prescribed regimen for this patient."},{"id":"D","text":"The last meropenem patient’s preparation and dosing interval","explanation":"Another patient may have had a different product, indication, and renal function."}],"point":"Verify the meropenem IV product, indication, renal function, and order before preparation.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-meropenem--standard--meropenem_iv_preparation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-meropenem::meropenem_iv_preparation"],"answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":9},{"id":"ibd2-meropenem--advanced","itemId":"ibd2-meropenem","itemType":"medication","title":"MEROPENEM","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"Before MEROPENEM, which prior antibiotic reaction carries the strongest immediate hypersensitivity concern?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Rapid wheeze and collapse after a beta-lactam","explanation":"The timing and cardiorespiratory involvement suggest a serious immediate reaction."},{"id":"B","text":"Metallic taste during metronidazole therapy","explanation":"This is a different, commonly nonallergic adverse effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Diarrhea several days into clindamycin therapy","explanation":"This needs review but is not the same immediate reaction pattern."},{"id":"D","text":"Local IV discomfort without systemic symptoms","explanation":"This is less specific for a serious immediate systemic allergy."}],"format