2-antagonist/prokinetic antiemetic.","id":"section-0-b20c166e7246ce79"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Blocks dopamine D2 receptors and promotes upper-GI motility, reducing nausea and vomiting.","id":"section-1-87a070623629f746"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Antiemetic/prokinetic for nausea and vomiting; now also ward-observed in IBD2, where postoperative nausea/vomiting is a common practical context.","id":"section-2-5fe5afe3027c6122"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"The local METO-ALISON ampoule contains 10 mg/2 mL = 5 mg/mL for IV or IM use. For ordinary small IV antiemetic doses, administration should be slow rather than a rapid push; use the actual ampoule insert for the exact minimum time.","id":"section-3-a30fa3ec44dddd16"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Watch for acute dystonia, akathisia/restlessness and other extrapyramidal reactions, especially after parenteral dosing. • Stop/escalate for sustained neck/jaw/eye deviation, severe restlessness, rigidity or other acute abnormal movements. • Avoid routine use in known mechanical GI obstruction/perforation or where stimulating motility is unsafe. • Parkinsonian symptoms can worsen because of dopamine blockade. • Rapid IV administration can make restlessness/anxiety more prominent.","id":"section-4-19ef6d8076dede9e"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"D2 blockade helps nausea and gastric emptying, but the same dopamine blockade can produce acute movement disorders.","id":"section-5-65fa7dfe983df65e"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"YES. The Palestinian spelling is PRAMIN. A locally registered product, METO-ALISON 10 mg/2 mL, is IV/IM; Palestinian data also list metoclopramide 10-mg ampoules and tablets. ED shelf/drawer label photographed, 2 Nov 2026. This is a named compartment, not an independently audited stock count; check the package before use.","id":"