y with other nephrotoxic drugs. • Review cultures and clinical response.","why":"Vancomycin inhibits gram-positive bacterial cell-wall synthesis. Exposure that is too low can fail therapy, while excessive exposure and nephrotoxic combinations increase kidney-injury risk.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Exact vial/ready-to-use formulation and concentration were not recorded; verify the product, concentration and order."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Glycopeptide antibacterial.","id":"section-0-555f8b3bf4d48232"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Binds D-Ala-D-Ala termini of peptidoglycan precursors and inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis.","id":"section-1-cee32b1638eae996"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Serious susceptible gram-positive infections, including MRSA when appropriate. In orthopaedics it may be used for severe wound, bone/joint or hardware-associated infection depending on cultures, local resistance patterns and the treatment plan.","id":"section-2-fc3e7decb4bc771e"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"IV for systemic infection. Use the exact product-specific dilution/infusion instructions and local protocol. Rapid infusion can cause a vancomycin infusion reaction and hypotension, so do not give as an unmonitored rapid IV push.","id":"section-3-eb9857dcd10181f3"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Monitor renal function and urine output. • Therapeutic drug monitoring may be required for systemic therapy; follow the hospital/pharmacy protocol. • Watch for flushing, pruritus, erythema and hypotension during infusion; slow/stop and escalate according to protocol if a significant infusion reaction occurs. • Monitor for nephrotoxicity, especially with other nephrotoxic drugs. • Review cultures and clinical response.","id":"section-4-93ec863c7b638d0d"},{"field":"why","label":"Essen