ections, but potentially disabling adverse effects make indication and monitoring important.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex in Oct 2026. Palestinian MoH records LEVOX 500 mg and 750 mg film-coated tablets containing levofloxacin; verify the exact strength in hand."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Fluoroquinolone antibacterial.","id":"section-0-b14861eab9097aed"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV, blocking DNA replication.","id":"section-1-925f25e9d618d5d4"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infections. Palestinian product information includes respiratory, urinary/kidney, and skin/soft-tissue infections; use should match the actual infection, culture data and local antimicrobial plan.","id":"section-2-9bc679b584458c73"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"For the observed LEVOX tablet, give PO exactly as ordered. Separate oral levofloxacin from products containing multivalent cations such as iron, zinc, magnesium/aluminum antacids, or sucralfate according to the product instructions because absorption can be reduced. Renal impairment may require dose/interval adjustment.","id":"section-3-8cfd3d6148b41a14"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Important fluoroquinolone warnings include tendinitis/tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy and CNS effects. • Stop/escalate new tendon pain or swelling rather than encouraging exercise through it. • Review renal function. • Watch for QT-prolongation risk, dysglycemia and significant diarrhea/C. difficile where relevant. • Use only for a susceptible bacterial indication; avoid unnecessary broad-spectrum exposure.","id":"section-4-f7ef9e0c992e73e7"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Levofloxacin inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Its 