ation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which new finding in a patient taking KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine most strongly suggests an upper-GI complication?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Black, tarry stool","explanation":"Melena can indicate upper gastrointestinal bleeding."},{"id":"B","text":"Pale, greasy stool","explanation":"This points toward a different gastrointestinal process."},{"id":"C","text":"Dry, hard stool","explanation":"This suggests constipation rather than characteristic upper-GI blood loss."},{"id":"D","text":"Small, formed stool","explanation":"This does not identify the bleeding pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_gi"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which situation makes the renal safety of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine particularly important to reassess?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Improved mobility with controlled pain","explanation":"This reflects recovery rather than increased renal risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Improved appetite with stable weight","explanation":"This does not identify a new renal-perfusion concern."},{"id":"C","text":"Vomiting with volume depletion","explanation":"Reduced effective circulating volume increases vulnerability to NSAID renal effects."},{"id":"D","text":"Stable intake with unchanged creatinine","explanation":"This does not suggest the same new renal vulnerability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_renal"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Which previous reaction most directly changes the hypersensitivity assessment before KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Bronchospasm after aspirin","explanation":"This may indicate a significant reaction relevant to another NSAID."},{"id":"B","text":"Nausea after an opioid","explanation":"This is a different drug effect and does not identify NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"C","text":"Flushing during vancomycin infusion","explanation":"This is a different drug-associated reaction."},{"id":"D","text":"Dry cough during an ACE inhibitor","explanation":"This is a different treatment-related effect."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_allergy"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"For adult ketorolac, what is the labeled maximum combined duration of parenteral and oral treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Fourteen days","explanation":"This is not the ketorolac limit."},{"id":"B","text":"Five days","explanation":"The combined treatment course should not exceed five days."},{"id":"C","text":"Twenty-one days","explanation":"This substantially exceeds the short-course limit."},{"id":"D","text":"Ten days","explanation":"This exceeds the labeled combined-duration limit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Ketorolac total treatment duration is limited.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--ketorolac_duration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::ketorolac_duration"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."},{"id":"B","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"C","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."},{"id":"D","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::pain_response"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"A patient is prescribed KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine and reports taking an additional NSAID bought outside the hospital. Which issue needs clarification?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A reduction in total COX-inhibitor exposure","explanation":"Adding another NSAID does not reduce the shared exposure."},{"id":"B","text":"Overlapping NSAID exposure and adverse-effect risk","explanation":"Two NSAIDs can duplicate GI and renal risk without being an intended combination."},{"id":"C","text":"A need to add an antiplatelet indication","explanation":"Taking an NSAID does not establish such an indication."},{"id":"D","text":"A need to interpret the combination as opioid tolerance","explanation":"Neither the report nor the drug group establishes opioid tolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--comprehensive--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::duplicate_nsaid"],"answerKey":"B"}],"questionCount":9,"model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":5,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac","title":"KETOROLAC TROMETHAMINE INJECTION, SOLUTION KETROLAC TROMETHAMINE (KETOROLAC TROMETHAMINE) INJECTION, SOLUTION [ANTHEA PHARMA PRIVATE LIMITED]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=7a7c8579-d423-4699-9435-69c4d127c654"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"NSAID analgesic.","mechanism":"Nonselectively inhibits cyclooxygenase (COX-1/COX-2), reducing prostaglandin synthesis.","use":"Short-term treatment of moderately severe acute pain, often postoperative or trauma-related. Current ketorolac labeling limits total therapy duration to no more than 5 days in adults.","administration":"Use the exact ordered formulation and route. Current injectable ketorolac products may be IV or IM depending on strength/product; do not infer route from the generic name alone. Oral ketorolac, where used, is continuation therapy after IV/IM treatment rather than a separate long-course analgesic.","monitor":"• Assess pain before and after administration. • Watch for GI bleeding, abdominal pain, melena/hematemesis and other bleeding. • Review renal function, hydration and urine output. • Do not combine casually with another NSAID such as ibuprofen. • Review bleeding risk and perioperative context. • Escalate severe GI symptoms, bleeding, anaphylaxis/bronchospasm, marked fall in urine output, or suspected renal injury.","why":"Ketorolac inhibits COX enzymes and prostaglandin synthesis, producing strong non-opioid analgesia but also GI, platelet and renal risks.","availability":"WARD-OBSERVED in IBD2 at Nasser Medical Complex on 3 Oct 2026. Ketrolac is a ketorolac tromethamine brand; an injectable Ketrolac product is marketed as 30 mg/mL, but verify the exact ward product, strength and route before administration. ED departmental stock confirmed by learner, 2 Nov 2026; verify actual injectable ampoule and ordered route."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"NSAID analgesic.","id":"section-0-71667bd9507765ae"},{"field":"mecha