wer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Treatment of susceptible bacterial infection","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Short-term treatment of acute pain","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Short-term treatment of moderately severe acute pain, often postoperative or trauma-related. Current ketorolac labeling limits total therapy duration to no more than 5 days in adults."},{"id":"C","text":"Prevention of valve-related thromboembolism","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement therapy for an adrenal crisis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::treatment-role"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--treatment-role","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which new finding in a patient taking KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine most strongly suggests an upper-GI complication?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pale, greasy stool","explanation":"This points toward a different gastrointestinal process."},{"id":"B","text":"Small, formed stool","explanation":"This does not identify the bleeding pattern."},{"id":"C","text":"Dry, hard stool","explanation":"This suggests constipation rather than characteristic upper-GI blood loss."},{"id":"D","text":"Black, tarry stool","explanation":"Melena can indicate upper gastrointestinal bleeding."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize gastrointestinal bleeding during NSAID treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_gi","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_gi"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_gi","answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which situation makes the renal safety of KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine particularly important to reassess?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Improved mobility with controlled pain","explanation":"This reflects recovery rather than increased renal risk."},{"id":"B","text":"Vomiting with volume depletion","explanation":"Reduced effective circulating volume increases vulnerability to NSAID renal effects."},{"id":"C","text":"Stable intake with unchanged creatinine","explanation":"This does not suggest the same new renal vulnerability."},{"id":"D","text":"Improved appetite with stable weight","explanation":"This does not identify a new renal-perfusion concern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Volume depletion increases NSAID-associated renal risk.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_renal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_renal"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_renal","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which previous reaction most directly changes the hypersensitivity assessment before KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Nausea after an opioid","explanation":"This is a different drug effect and does not identify NSAID hypersensitivity."},{"id":"B","text":"Dry cough during an ACE inhibitor","explanation":"This is a different treatment-related effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Bronchospasm after aspirin","explanation":"This may indicate a significant reaction relevant to another NSAID."},{"id":"D","text":"Flushing during vancomycin infusion","explanation":"This is a different drug-associated reaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Review prior aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity and bronchospasm.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--nsaid_allergy","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::nsaid_allergy"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--nsaid_allergy","answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"For adult ketorolac, what is the labeled maximum combined duration of parenteral and oral treatment?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Twenty-one days","explanation":"This substantially exceeds the short-course limit."},{"id":"B","text":"Five days","explanation":"The combined treatment course should not exceed five days."},{"id":"C","text":"Fourteen days","explanation":"This is not the ketorolac limit."},{"id":"D","text":"Ten days","explanation":"This exceeds the labeled combined-duration limit."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Ketorolac total treatment duration is limited.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--ketorolac_duration","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::ketorolac_duration"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--ketorolac_duration","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which paired reassessment directly measures the intended analgesic response to KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain intensity and ability to perform the agreed activity","explanation":"These compare pain and function with baseline."},{"id":"B","text":"Alertness and respiratory effort during rest","explanation":"These assess important adverse effects rather than analgesic benefit itself."},{"id":"C","text":"Stool frequency and abdominal discomfort","explanation":"These assess bowel effects rather than analgesic benefit."},{"id":"D","text":"BP and pulse during a postural change","explanation":"These assess hemodynamic tolerance rather than the pain response."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Assess the intended analgesic or antipyretic response before and after treatment.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--pain_response","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::pain_response"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--standard--pain_response","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A patient is prescribed KETROLAC — ketorolac tromethamine and reports taking an additional NSAID bought outside the hospital. Which issue needs clarification?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"A need to add an antiplatelet indication","explanation":"Taking an NSAID does not establish such an indication."},{"id":"B","text":"A reduction in total COX-inhibitor exposure","explanation":"Adding another NSAID does not reduce the shared exposure."},{"id":"C","text":"Overlapping NSAID exposure and adverse-effect risk","explanation":"Two NSAIDs can duplicate GI and renal risk without being an intended combination."},{"id":"D","text":"A need to interpret the combination as opioid tolerance","explanation":"Neither the report nor the drug group establishes opioid tolerance."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Avoid unintended duplication of NSAIDs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--7-day--duplicate_nsaid","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac::duplicate_nsaid"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ibd2-ketrolac-ketorolac--advanced--duplicate_nsaid","answerKey":"C"}],"questionCount":9}],"scoringExclusions":[]},{"itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in hepatic production follows warfarin’s inhibition of vitamin K recycling?","choices":[{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Warfarin reduces vitamin K-dependent activation of these coagulation factors and natural anticoagulants.","id":"A"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"Proteins C and S are also vitamin K dependent.","id":"B"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Factors V and VIII are not part of the vitamin K-dependent set. This omits VII and IX.","id":"C"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"The clotting-factor set is incorrect and proteins C and S are also affected.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the complete vitamin K-dependent factor and protein pattern.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which anticoagulant class contains warfarin?","choices":[{"text":"Direct factor Xa inhibitor","explanation":"This is the class of drugs such as apixaban.","id":"A"},{"text":"Direct thrombin inhibitor","explanation":"This describes drugs such as dabigatran.","id":"B"},{"text":"Vitamin K antagonist","explanation":"Warfarin acts through inhibition of vitamin K recycling.","id":"C"},{"text":"Antithrombin-dependent heparin","explanation":"UFH and LMWH act through antithrombin rather than inhibiting vitamin K recycling.","id":"D"}],"point":"Differentiate the vitamin K antagonist from common direct and indirect anticoagulants.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a mechanical aortic prosthesis has completed the immediate postoperative phase. Which long-term anticoagulant plan is consistent with the valve indication?","choices":[{"text":"Oral apixaban with renal function monitoring","explanation":"Direct oral anticoagulants are not recommended for mechanical prosthetic valves.","id":"A"},{"text":"Daily aspirin with platelet count monitoring","explanation":"Aspirin does not replace VKA anticoagulation for the mechanical valve.","id":"B"},{"text":"Oral warfarin with INR monitoring","explanation":"Mechanical prosthetic valves require monitored long-term VKA anticoagulation unless a specialist determines an exceptional plan.","id":"C"},{"text":"Subcutaneous enoxaparin with creatinine clearance monitoring","explanation":"LMWH can be used for bridging. It is not the guideline’s routine long-term maintenance strategy for this valve.","id":"D"}],"point":"Choose an oral VKA for mechanical valve anticoagulation.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a mechanical valve receives both warfarin and an IV UFH bridge. Which result tracks the effect of warfarin for eventual transition to oral therapy?","choices":[{"text":"aPTT","explanation":"aPTT is used in some protocols for UFH, not to assess warfarin intensity.","id":"A"},{"text":"INR","explanation":"INR measures warfarin effect and is compared with the prescribed valve target.","id":"B"},{"text":"Platelet count","explanation":"Platelets can help detect complications but do not measure warfarin intensity.","id":"C"},{"text":"Hemoglobin","explanation":"Hemoglobin can help assess blood loss but does not measure warfarin intensity.","id":"D"}],"point":"Use INR to assess the intensity of warfarin anticoagulation.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which intervention restores vitamin K-dependent factor production during protocol-directed warfarin reversal?","choices":[{"text":"Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate","explanation":"This rapidly replaces circulating factors. It does not itself restore their hepatic production.","id":"A"},{"text":"Vitamin K","explanation":"Restores availability for hepatic production of functional factors. The clinical protocol determines whether rapid factor replacement is also indicated.","id":"B"},{"text":"Protamine","explanation":"Protamine neutralizes heparin, rather than restoring vitamin K-dependent production.","id":"C"},{"text":"Idarucizumab","explanation":"This binds dabigatran rather than acting on vitamin K recycling.","id":"D"}],"point":"Distinguish vitamin K from factor replacement and anticoagulant-specific antidotes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_reversal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_reversal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Warfarin and therapeutic heparin were started after mechanical-valve implantation. Today is the first day the INR reaches its prescribed range. Which finding supports stopping the bridge under the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR remains in the prescribed range on a second consecutive day.","explanation":"ESC/EACTS describes two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before stopping the bridge."},{"id":"B","text":"INR reaches the lower edge of its range on the first day.","explanation":"One in-range day does not meet this criterion."},{"id":"C","text":"Two oral warfarin doses have been given and the INR is still rising.","explanation":"Number of doses and direction of change do not establish two days of therapeutic INR."},{"id":"D","text":"The aPTT reaches the heparin goal on two consecutive days.","explanation":"aPTT can reflect UFH effect, but does not establish sustained therapeutic VKA effect."}],"point":"After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS uses two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before ending the heparin bridge.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_bridge_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_stop"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stable on warfarin plans to eat more leafy greens for health. What advice best supports stable anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Offset extra leafy greens by taking an extra warfarin dose that evening.","explanation":"Self-adjusting warfarin to individual meals risks unstable anticoagulation."},{"id":"B","text":"Keep vitamin K intake consistent and discuss the planned change with the anticoagulation team.","explanation":"A stable intake supports stable INR. The team can advise on follow-up if intake changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Avoid leafy greens on every day that warfarin is scheduled.","explanation":"Routine avoidance is unnecessary. Consistency matters."},{"id":"D","text":"Move leafy greens to the morning and take warfarin at night to avoid the effect.","explanation":"Separating the times does not remove the effect of vitamin K intake on INR."}],"point":"Keep dietary vitamin K consistent on warfarin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After mechanical-valve implantation, the team considers postoperative hemostasis adequate for anticoagulation at 18 hours. Which initiation plan matches the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Begin the VKA now and add therapeutic heparin only once INR is in range","explanation":"The bridge covers the interval before a stable therapeutic INR. Waiting defeats that purpose."},{"id":"B","text":"Begin the VKA and prophylactic-dose heparin now","explanation":"The mechanical-valve bridge uses therapeutic anticoagulation when safe."},{"id":"C","text":"Begin therapeutic heparin now and postpone the VKA for a fixed week","explanation":"Postpone only if clinically needed. The pathway starts the VKA with the bridge within the safe window."},{"id":"D","text":"Begin the VKA together with therapeutic UFH or LMWH now","explanation":"The pathway recommends both within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe."}],"point":"Start a VKA with therapeutic UFH or LMWH within 24 hours or as soon as safe.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_bridge_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_start"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking warfarin has a new drop in blood pressure. Which additional finding most directly supports bleeding as a contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New polyuria with clear urine","explanation":"A fluid loss may contribute to hypotension, but clear polyuria does not specifically point to hemorrhage."},{"id":"B","text":"New slow pulse after an AV-nodal blocker without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This could support a medication-related hemodynamic effect. It does not specifically support bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"New fever and rigors without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This pattern may point toward infection rather than specifically toward bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"New melena or red urine","explanation":"These can reflect gastrointestinal or urinary bleeding. New hypotension increases urgency."}],"point":"Recognize urinary or gastrointestinal blood as a red flag with hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_occult_bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_occult_bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on warfarin develops a sudden severe headache and new arm weakness. Yesterday’s INR was in range. Which response is appropriate?","choices":[{"text":"Activate the urgent stroke pathway and report warfarin exposure and the last INR.","explanation":"Intracranial bleeding is possible despite an INR in range. New focal findings need immediate evaluation.","id":"A"},{"text":"Request a repeat INR and wait for the result before activating the stroke pathway.","explanation":"An INR result cannot establish the cause or safely delay assessment.","id":"B"},{"text":"Use yesterday’s INR to prioritize an ischemic diagnosis and defer bleeding evaluation.","explanation":"A therapeutic INR does not exclude intracranial bleeding.","id":"C"},{"text":"Request vitamin K based on headache alone before assessing the neurological deficit.","explanation":"Immediate stroke assessment helps establish the cause and guide reversal if indicated.","id":"D"}],"point":"New focal neurological signs on warfarin require emergency evaluation even with a recent INR in range.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which onset and peak profile fits a single oral dose of warfarin in its prescribing information?","choices":[{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect about 72–96 hours","explanation":"Existing functional factors decline over time. The peak after one dose does not establish a stable therapeutic INR.","id":"A"},{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect in the same 24 hours","explanation":"Early measurable effect does not mean that peak anticoagulant effect has been reached.","id":"B"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak effect the same day","explanation":"Some anticoagulant effect can occur substantially earlier than 72–96 hours.","id":"C"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak a further week later","explanation":"This delays both the usual onset and single-dose peak beyond the teaching reference.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recall early onset, delayed peak, and the distinction from time to stable INR.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a previously stable therapeutic INR starts an antibiotic that can interact with warfarin and has an acute febrile illness. Which monitoring plan most directly evaluates a change in anticoagulation?","choices":[{"text":"Arrange aPTT monitoring and assess for bleeding","explanation":"aPTT is not used to guide warfarin therapy.","id":"A"},{"text":"Arrange a CBC alone and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"CBC helps detect blood loss but does not quantify warfarin effect.","id":"B"},{"text":"Arrange anti-Xa monitoring and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"Anti-Xa is not the warfarin monitoring test.","id":"C"},{"text":"Arrange an earlier INR check and assess for bleeding","explanation":"Both the new medicine and the illness can change INR. Follow the anticoagulation service’s monitoring plan.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the need to reassess INR with medication and illness changes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which findings can justify different prescribed INR targets for two patients with mechanical heart valves?","choices":[{"text":"Baseline INR before the first dose plus the scheduled dose time","explanation":"Baseline INR helps assess the patient but does not define the valve-specific treatment target.","id":"A"},{"text":"Warfarin tablet strength plus total milligrams prescribed per week","explanation":"Dose is adjusted to reach the target. It does not set that target.","id":"B"},{"text":"Prosthesis type and position plus patient thromboembolic risk","explanation":"Targets are individualized using valve and patient risk characteristics.","id":"C"},{"text":"Bridging agent and its current monitoring result","explanation":"These guide the bridge but do not set the INR target for long-term valve protection.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recognize valve type, position and thromboembolic risk as target determinants.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--mechanical_valve","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::mechanical_valve"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which mechanism explains why a functioning mechanical heart valve still creates a long-term thromboembolic risk?","choices":[{"text":"A non-endothelial surface but normal local flow makes embolism the only concern","explanation":"Abnormal flow contributes to thrombogenicity, and both valve thrombosis and embolism matter.","id":"A"},{"text":"A non-endothelial surface and disturbed blood flow favor thrombus formation","explanation":"Prosthetic surface contact and abnormal local flow contribute to valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","id":"B"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and disturbed flow make platelet contact irrelevant","explanation":"The mechanical prosthesis is not a native endothelial surface, and platelet activation can contribute.","id":"C"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and normal local flow cause thrombosis through vitamin K depletion","explanation":"Vitamin K depletion is not the mechanism of prosthetic thrombogenicity.","id":"D"}],"point":"Connect the prosthetic surface and disturbed flow to thrombosis and embolization.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--valve_thrombosis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::valve_thrombosis"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"warfarin-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in hepatic production follows warfarin’s inhibition of vitamin K recycling?","choices":[{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Warfarin reduces vitamin K-dependent activation of these coagulation factors and natural anticoagulants.","id":"A"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"Proteins C and S are also vitamin K dependent.","id":"B"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Factors V and VIII are not part of the vitamin K-dependent set. This omits VII and IX.","id":"C"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"The clotting-factor set is incorrect and proteins C and S are also affected.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the complete vitamin K-dependent factor and protein pattern.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which anticoagulant class contains warfarin?","choices":[{"text":"Direct factor Xa inhibitor","explanation":"This is the class of drugs such as apixaban.","id":"A"},{"text":"Direct thrombin inhibitor","explanation":"This describes drugs such as dabigatran.","id":"B"},{"text":"Vitamin K antagonist","explanation":"Warfarin acts through inhibition of vitamin K recycling.","id":"C"},{"text":"Antithrombin-dependent heparin","explanation":"UFH and LMWH act through antithrombin rather than inhibiting vitamin K recycling.","id":"D"}],"point":"Differentiate the vitamin K antagonist from common direct and indirect anticoagulants.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a mechanical aortic prosthesis has completed the immediate postoperative phase. Which long-term anticoagulant plan is consistent with the valve indication?","choices":[{"text":"Oral apixaban with renal function monitoring","explanation":"Direct oral anticoagulants are not recommended for mechanical prosthetic valves.","id":"A"},{"text":"Daily aspirin with platelet count monitoring","explanation":"Aspirin does not replace VKA anticoagulation for the mechanical valve.","id":"B"},{"text":"Oral warfarin with INR monitoring","explanation":"Mechanical prosthetic valves require monitored long-term VKA anticoagulation unless a specialist determines an exceptional plan.","id":"C"},{"text":"Subcutaneous enoxaparin with creatinine clearance monitoring","explanation":"LMWH can be used for bridging. It is not the guideline’s routine long-term maintenance strategy for this valve.","id":"D"}],"point":"Choose an oral VKA for mechanical valve anticoagulation.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a mechanical valve receives both warfarin and an IV UFH bridge. Which result tracks the effect of warfarin for eventual transition to oral therapy?","choices":[{"text":"aPTT","explanation":"aPTT is used in some protocols for UFH, not to assess warfarin intensity.","id":"A"},{"text":"INR","explanation":"INR measures warfarin effect and is compared with the prescribed valve target.","id":"B"},{"text":"Platelet count","explanation":"Platelets can help detect complications but do not measure warfarin intensity.","id":"C"},{"text":"Hemoglobin","explanation":"Hemoglobin can help assess blood loss but does not measure warfarin intensity.","id":"D"}],"point":"Use INR to assess the intensity of warfarin anticoagulation.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which intervention restores vitamin K-dependent factor production during protocol-directed warfarin reversal?","choices":[{"text":"Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate","explanation":"This rapidly replaces circulating factors. It does not itself restore their hepatic production.","id":"A"},{"text":"Vitamin K","explanation":"Restores availability for hepatic production of functional factors. The clinical protocol determines whether rapid factor replacement is also indicated.","id":"B"},{"text":"Protamine","explanation":"Protamine neutralizes heparin, rather than restoring vitamin K-dependent production.","id":"C"},{"text":"Idarucizumab","explanation":"This binds dabigatran rather than acting on vitamin K recycling.","id":"D"}],"point":"Distinguish vitamin K from factor replacement and anticoagulant-specific antidotes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_reversal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_reversal"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Warfarin and therapeutic heparin were started after mechanical-valve implantation. Today is the first day the INR reaches its prescribed range. Which finding supports stopping the bridge under the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR remains in the prescribed range on a second consecutive day.","explanation":"ESC/EACTS describes two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before stopping the bridge."},{"id":"B","text":"INR reaches the lower edge of its range on the first day.","explanation":"One in-range day does not meet this criterion."},{"id":"C","text":"Two oral warfarin doses have been given and the INR is still rising.","explanation":"Number of doses and direction of change do not establish two days of therapeutic INR."},{"id":"D","text":"The aPTT reaches the heparin goal on two consecutive days.","explanation":"aPTT can reflect UFH effect, but does not establish sustained therapeutic VKA effect."}],"point":"After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS uses two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before ending the heparin bridge.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_bridge_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_stop"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stable on warfarin plans to eat more leafy greens for health. What advice best supports stable anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Offset extra leafy greens by taking an extra warfarin dose that evening.","explanation":"Self-adjusting warfarin to individual meals risks unstable anticoagulation."},{"id":"B","text":"Keep vitamin K intake consistent and discuss the planned change with the anticoagulation team.","explanation":"A stable intake supports stable INR. The team can advise on follow-up if intake changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Avoid leafy greens on every day that warfarin is scheduled.","explanation":"Routine avoidance is unnecessary. Consistency matters."},{"id":"D","text":"Move leafy greens to the morning and take warfarin at night to avoid the effect.","explanation":"Separating the times does not remove the effect of vitamin K intake on INR."}],"point":"Keep dietary vitamin K consistent on warfarin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After mechanical-valve implantation, the team considers postoperative hemostasis adequate for anticoagulation at 18 hours. Which initiation plan matches the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Begin the VKA now and add therapeutic heparin only once INR is in range","explanation":"The bridge covers the interval before a stable therapeutic INR. Waiting defeats that purpose."},{"id":"B","text":"Begin the VKA and prophylactic-dose heparin now","explanation":"The mechanical-valve bridge uses therapeutic anticoagulation when safe."},{"id":"C","text":"Begin therapeutic heparin now and postpone the VKA for a fixed week","explanation":"Postpone only if clinically needed. The pathway starts the VKA with the bridge within the safe window."},{"id":"D","text":"Begin the VKA together with therapeutic UFH or LMWH now","explanation":"The pathway recommends both within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe."}],"point":"Start a VKA with therapeutic UFH or LMWH within 24 hours or as soon as safe.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_bridge_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_start"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking warfarin has a new drop in blood pressure. Which additional finding most directly supports bleeding as a contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New polyuria with clear urine","explanation":"A fluid loss may contribute to hypotension, but clear polyuria does not specifically point to hemorrhage."},{"id":"B","text":"New slow pulse after an AV-nodal blocker without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This could support a medication-related hemodynamic effect. It does not specifically support bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"New fever and rigors without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This pattern may point toward infection rather than specifically toward bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"New melena or red urine","explanation":"These can reflect gastrointestinal or urinary bleeding. New hypotension increases urgency."}],"point":"Recognize urinary or gastrointestinal blood as a red flag with hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_occult_bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_occult_bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on warfarin develops a sudden severe headache and new arm weakness. Yesterday’s INR was in range. Which response is appropriate?","choices":[{"text":"Activate the urgent stroke pathway and report warfarin exposure and the last INR.","explanation":"Intracranial bleeding is possible despite an INR in range. New focal findings need immediate evaluation.","id":"A"},{"text":"Request a repeat INR and wait for the result before activating the stroke pathway.","explanation":"An INR result cannot establish the cause or safely delay assessment.","id":"B"},{"text":"Use yesterday’s INR to prioritize an ischemic diagnosis and defer bleeding evaluation.","explanation":"A therapeutic INR does not exclude intracranial bleeding.","id":"C"},{"text":"Request vitamin K based on headache alone before assessing the neurological deficit.","explanation":"Immediate stroke assessment helps establish the cause and guide reversal if indicated.","id":"D"}],"point":"New focal neurological signs on warfarin require emergency evaluation even with a recent INR in range.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which onset and peak profile fits a single oral dose of warfarin in its prescribing information?","choices":[{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect about 72–96 hours","explanation":"Existing functional factors decline over time. The peak after one dose does not establish a stable therapeutic INR.","id":"A"},{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect in the same 24 hours","explanation":"Early measurable effect does not mean that peak anticoagulant effect has been reached.","id":"B"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak effect the same day","explanation":"Some anticoagulant effect can occur substantially earlier than 72–96 hours.","id":"C"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak a further week later","explanation":"This delays both the usual onset and single-dose peak beyond the teaching reference.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recall early onset, delayed peak, and the distinction from time to stable INR.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a previously stable therapeutic INR starts an antibiotic that can interact with warfarin and has an acute febrile illness. Which monitoring plan most directly evaluates a change in anticoagulation?","choices":[{"text":"Arrange aPTT monitoring and assess for bleeding","explanation":"aPTT is not used to guide warfarin therapy.","id":"A"},{"text":"Arrange a CBC alone and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"CBC helps detect blood loss but does not quantify warfarin effect.","id":"B"},{"text":"Arrange anti-Xa monitoring and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"Anti-Xa is not the warfarin monitoring test.","id":"C"},{"text":"Arrange an earlier INR check and assess for bleeding","explanation":"Both the new medicine and the illness can change INR. Follow the anticoagulation service’s monitoring plan.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the need to reassess INR with medication and illness changes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which findings can justify different prescribed INR targets for two patients with mechanical heart valves?","choices":[{"text":"Baseline INR before the first dose plus the scheduled dose time","explanation":"Baseline INR helps assess the patient but does not define the valve-specific treatment target.","id":"A"},{"text":"Warfarin tablet strength plus total milligrams prescribed per week","explanation":"Dose is adjusted to reach the target. It does not set that target.","id":"B"},{"text":"Prosthesis type and position plus patient thromboembolic risk","explanation":"Targets are individualized using valve and patient risk characteristics.","id":"C"},{"text":"Bridging agent and its current monitoring result","explanation":"These guide the bridge but do not set the INR target for long-term valve protection.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recognize valve type, position and thromboembolic risk as target determinants.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--mechanical_valve","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::mechanical_valve"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which mechanism explains why a functioning mechanical heart valve still creates a long-term thromboembolic risk?","choices":[{"text":"A non-endothelial surface but normal local flow makes embolism the only concern","explanation":"Abnormal flow contributes to thrombogenicity, and both valve thrombosis and embolism matter.","id":"A"},{"text":"A non-endothelial surface and disturbed blood flow favor thrombus formation","explanation":"Prosthetic surface contact and abnormal local flow contribute to valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","id":"B"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and disturbed flow make platelet contact irrelevant","explanation":"The mechanical prosthesis is not a native endothelial surface, and platelet activation can contribute.","id":"C"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and normal local flow cause thrombosis through vitamin K depletion","explanation":"Vitamin K depletion is not the mechanism of prosthetic thrombogenicity.","id":"D"}],"point":"Connect the prosthetic surface and disturbed flow to thrombosis and embolization.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--valve_thrombosis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::valve_thrombosis"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"warfarin-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--21-day","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"21-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":2},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which change in hepatic production follows warfarin’s inhibition of vitamin K recycling?","choices":[{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Warfarin reduces vitamin K-dependent activation of these coagulation factors and natural anticoagulants.","id":"A"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, VII, IX and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"Proteins C and S are also vitamin K dependent.","id":"B"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X and proteins C and S","explanation":"Factors V and VIII are not part of the vitamin K-dependent set. This omits VII and IX.","id":"C"},{"text":"Reduced functional factors II, V, VIII and X with unchanged proteins C and S","explanation":"The clotting-factor set is incorrect and proteins C and S are also affected.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the complete vitamin K-dependent factor and protein pattern.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--principal-action","points"