mprehensive--warfarin_bridge_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_start"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking warfarin has a new drop in blood pressure. Which additional finding most directly supports bleeding as a contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New polyuria with clear urine","explanation":"A fluid loss may contribute to hypotension, but clear polyuria does not specifically point to hemorrhage."},{"id":"B","text":"New slow pulse after an AV-nodal blocker without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This could support a medication-related hemodynamic effect. It does not specifically support bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"New fever and rigors without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This pattern may point toward infection rather than specifically toward bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"New melena or red urine","explanation":"These can reflect gastrointestinal or urinary bleeding. New hypotension increases urgency."}],"point":"Recognize urinary or gastrointestinal blood as a red flag with hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_occult_bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_occult_bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient on warfarin develops a sudden severe headache and new arm weakness. Yesterday’s INR was in range. Which response is appropriate?","choices":[{"text":"Activate the urgent stroke pathway and report warfarin exposure and the last INR.","explanation":"Intracranial bleeding is possible despite an INR in range. New focal findings need immediate evaluation.","id":"A"},{"text":"Request a repeat INR and wait for the result before activating the stroke pathway.","explanation":"An INR result cannot establish the cause or safely delay assessment.","id":"B"},{"text":"Use yesterday’s INR to prioritize an ischemic diagnosis and defer bleeding evaluation.","explanation":"A therapeutic INR does not exclude intracranial bleeding.","id":"C"},{"text":"Request vitamin K based on headache alone before assessing the neurological deficit.","explanation":"Immediate stroke assessment helps establish the cause and guide reversal if indicated.","id":"D"}],"point":"New focal neurological signs on warfarin require emergency evaluation even with a recent INR in range.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which onset and peak profile fits a single oral dose of warfarin in its prescribing information?","choices":[{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect about 72–96 hours","explanation":"Existing functional factors decline over time. The peak after one dose does not establish a stable therapeutic INR.","id":"A"},{"text":"Effect can begin within 24 hours, with peak effect in the same 24 hours","explanation":"Early measurable effect does not mean that peak anticoagulant effect has been reached.","id":"B"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak effect the same day","explanation":"Some anticoagulant effect can occur substantially earlier than 72–96 hours.","id":"C"},{"text":"Effect first begins at 72–96 hours, with peak a further week later","explanation":"This delays both the usual onset and single-dose peak beyond the teaching reference.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recall early onset, delayed peak, and the distinction from time to stable INR.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with a previously stable therapeutic INR starts an antibiotic that can interact with warfarin and has an acute febrile illness. Which monitoring plan most directly evaluates a change in anticoagulation?","choices":[{"text":"Arrange aPTT monitoring and assess for bleeding","explanation":"aPTT is not used to guide warfarin therapy.","id":"A"},{"text":"Arrange a CBC alone and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"CBC helps detect blood loss but does not quantify warfarin effect.","id":"B"},{"text":"Arrange anti-Xa monitoring and wait for the routine INR appointment","explanation":"Anti-Xa is not the warfarin monitoring test.","id":"C"},{"text":"Arrange an earlier INR check and assess for bleeding","explanation":"Both the new medicine and the illness can change INR. Follow the anticoagulation service’s monitoring plan.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify the need to reassess INR with medication and illness changes.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which findings can justify different prescribed INR targets for two patients with mechanical heart valves?","choices":[{"text":"Baseline INR before the first dose plus the scheduled dose time","explanation":"Baseline INR helps assess the patient but does not define the valve-specific treatment target.","id":"A"},{"text":"Warfarin tablet strength plus total milligrams prescribed per week","explanation":"Dose is adjusted to reach the target. It does not set that target.","id":"B"},{"text":"Prosthesis type and position plus patient thromboembolic risk","explanation":"Targets are individualized using valve and patient risk characteristics.","id":"C"},{"text":"Bridging agent and its current monitoring result","explanation":"These guide the bridge but do not set the INR target for long-term valve protection.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recognize valve type, position and thromboembolic risk as target determinants.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--mechanical_valve","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::mechanical_valve"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which mechanism explains why a functioning mechanical heart valve still creates a long-term thromboembolic risk?","choices":[{"text":"A non-endothelial surface but normal local flow makes embolism the only concern","explanation":"Abnormal flow contributes to thrombogenicity, and both valve thrombosis and embolism matter.","id":"A"},{"text":"A non-endothelial surface and disturbed blood flow favor thrombus formation","explanation":"Prosthetic surface contact and abnormal local flow contribute to valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","id":"B"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and disturbed flow make platelet contact irrelevant","explanation":"The mechanical prosthesis is not a native endothelial surface, and platelet activation can contribute.","id":"C"},{"text":"An endothelial surface and normal local flow cause thrombosis through vitamin K depletion","explanation":"Vitamin K depletion is not the mechanism of prosthetic thrombogenicity.","id":"D"}],"point":"Connect the prosthetic surface and disturbed flow to thrombosis and embolization.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--comprehensive--valve_thrombosis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::valve_thrombosis"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["why"]}],"questionCount":14,"correctionRevision":"warfarin-source-review-20261115","model":"comprehensive-v1"}],"bankVersion":6,"legacyBankVersion":4,"sources":[{"id":"content-v35"},{"id":"label-ccu-warfarin-coumadin","title":"WARFARIN SODIUM TABLET [CARDINAL HEALTH 107, LLC]","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=cdbbc317-35d3-42ba-94f0-c83891358529"},{"id":"palestinian-eml-2024-antithrombotics","title":"Palestinian Essential Medicine List (2024)","url":"https://pharmacy.moh.ps/Content/Laws/Vuoci8KjRC8YZF8ICukjbSxQ_72NDdGOxPdwXuKgw68HDsmzo.pdf"},{"id":"esc-eacts-vhd-2025-anticoagulation","title":"2025 ESC/EACTS valvular heart disease guideline","url":"https://academic.oup.com/ejcts/article/67/8/ezaf276/8242828"},{"id":"review-label-ccu-warfarin-coumadin","title":"DailyMed Warfarin sodium","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b1bd0d02-eccb-4449-82b9-9b552835029e"}],"sourceFields":{"drugclass":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant.","mechanism":"Inhibits VKORC1, reducing regeneration of vitamin K and therefore synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S).","use":"Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement.","administration":"Give orally as ordered. Compare INR with the individual target, which depends on valve type, position and thromboembolic risk. After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS recommends starting therapeutic UFH or LMWH together with a VKA within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe. The team can stop the bridge after two consecutive days of INR in range.","monitor":"• Check INR and the prescribed target. • Assess for bleeding, including blood in urine or stool, new neurological symptoms and unexplained hypotension. • Check new medicines, including antibiotics, and report illness or major diet changes that may alter INR. Keep dietary vitamin K intake consistent. • Effect generally starts within 24 hours, with peak effect delayed 72–96 hours. Stable therapeutic INR can take longer. • Vitamin K can be used for reversal, with treatment determined by bleeding severity, INR and protocol.","why":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin-K recycling, reducing synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Mechanical valves are thrombogenic because blood contacts an artificial non-endothelial surface and experiences disturbed flow, promoting thrombus formation; anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","availability":"The Palestinian Essential Medicine List (2024) includes 5-mg warfarin tablets. Coumadin/warfarin was reported in Nasser Medical Complex CCU. Confirm the actual tablet strength and current stock."},"readingSections":[{"field":"drugclass","label":"Drug class","text":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant.","id":"section-0-d5ab4019e440ff56"},{"field":"mechanism","label":"Mechanism of action","text":"Inhibits VKORC1, reducing regeneration of vitamin K and therefore synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S).","id":"section-1-cb2ebc64244cec75"},{"field":"use","label":"Main use","text":"Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement.","id":"section-2-dffbfeb626e2fda3"},{"field":"administration","label":"Form & administration","text":"Give orally as ordered. Compare INR with the individual target, which depends on valve type, position and thromboembolic risk. After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS recommends starting therapeutic UFH or LMWH together with a VKA within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe. The team can stop the bridge after two consecutive days of INR in range.","id":"section-3-981ecb165ad4569d"},{"field":"monitor","label":"Nursing considerations","text":"• Check INR and the prescribed target. • Assess for bleeding, including blood in urine or stool, new neurological symptoms and unexplained hypotension. • Check new medicines, including antibiotics, and report illness or major diet changes that may alter INR. Keep dietary vitamin K intake consistent. • Effect generally starts within 24 hours, with peak effect delayed 72–96 hours. Stable therapeutic INR can take longer. • Vitamin K can be used for reversal, with treatment determined by bleeding severity, INR and protocol.","id":"section-4-304364309666ffab"},{"field":"why","label":"Essential rationale","text":"Warfarin inhibits vitamin-K recycling, reducing synthesis of vitamin-K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Mechanical valves are thrombogenic because blood contacts an artificial non-endothelial surface and experiences disturbed flow, promoting thrombus formation; anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and systemic embolic risk.","id":"section-5-cff7a468acc5301c"},{"field":"availability","label":"Palestine availability","text":"The Palestinian Essential Medicine List (2024) includes 5-mg warfarin tablets. Coumadin/warfarin was reported in Nasser Medical Complex CCU. Confirm the actual tablet strength and current stock.","id":"section-6-75cad278edf9f609"}],"legacyExams":[{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"standard","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">=","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"same standard form after required reading","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"B","text":"Relaxes prostate and urethral smooth muscle","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"C","text":"Changes inflammatory/stress-response gene transcription","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::principal-action"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does COUMADIN — warfarin belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unfractionated heparin","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"B","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"C","text":"Vitamin-K antagonist","explanation":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"D","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::drug-class"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Long-term anticoagulation for selected indications","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement."},{"id":"B","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::treatment-role"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which result is used to adjust warfarin against the prescribed therapeutic target?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Serum potassium","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify warfarin effect."},{"id":"B","text":"INR","explanation":"INR is the routine warfarin anticoagulation measure."},{"id":"C","text":"Platelet count","explanation":"This measures cell quantity, not the warfarin anticoagulation intensity."},{"id":"D","text":"aPTT","explanation":"This is used for other coagulation assessments and some UFH protocols, not routine warfarin targeting."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Warfarin is monitored against an individually prescribed INR target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"Which agent has a role in reversing warfarin’s effect under the appropriate clinical protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Naloxone","explanation":"It reverses opioid effects, not vitamin-K antagonism."},{"id":"B","text":"Vitamin K","explanation":"It supports restoration of vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis; the full reversal plan depends on severity."},{"id":"C","text":"Adenosine","explanation":"It transiently blocks AV-nodal conduction rather than reversing warfarin."},{"id":"D","text":"Atropine","explanation":"It treats selected bradycardia through muscarinic blockade."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Vitamin K has an indication-specific role in warfarin reversal.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_reversal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_reversal"],"answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Warfarin and therapeutic heparin were started after mechanical-valve implantation. Today is the first day the INR reaches its prescribed range. Which finding supports stopping the bridge under the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR remains in the prescribed range on a second consecutive day.","explanation":"ESC/EACTS describes two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before stopping the bridge."},{"id":"B","text":"INR reaches the lower edge of its range on the first day.","explanation":"One in-range day does not meet this criterion."},{"id":"C","text":"Two oral warfarin doses have been given and the INR is still rising.","explanation":"Number of doses and direction of change do not establish two days of therapeutic INR."},{"id":"D","text":"The aPTT reaches the heparin goal on two consecutive days.","explanation":"aPTT can reflect UFH effect, but does not establish sustained therapeutic VKA effect."}],"point":"After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS uses two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before ending the heparin bridge.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_bridge_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_stop"],"answerKey":"A"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stable on warfarin plans to eat more leafy greens for health. What advice best supports stable anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Offset extra leafy greens by taking an extra warfarin dose that evening.","explanation":"Self-adjusting warfarin to individual meals risks unstable anticoagulation."},{"id":"B","text":"Avoid leafy greens on every day that warfarin is scheduled.","explanation":"Routine avoidance is unnecessary. Consistency matters."},{"id":"C","text":"Keep vitamin K intake consistent and discuss the planned change with the anticoagulation team.","explanation":"A stable intake supports stable INR. The team can advise on follow-up if intake changes."},{"id":"D","text":"Move leafy greens to the morning and take warfarin at night to avoid the effect.","explanation":"Separating the times does not remove the effect of vitamin K intake on INR."}],"point":"Keep dietary vitamin K consistent on warfarin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After mechanical-valve implantation, the team considers postoperative hemostasis adequate for anticoagulation at 18 hours. Which initiation plan matches the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Begin the VKA now and add therapeutic heparin only once INR is in range","explanation":"The bridge covers the interval before a stable therapeutic INR. Waiting defeats that purpose."},{"id":"B","text":"Begin the VKA and prophylactic-dose heparin now","explanation":"The mechanical-valve bridge uses therapeutic anticoagulation when safe."},{"id":"C","text":"Begin therapeutic heparin now and postpone the VKA for a fixed week","explanation":"Postpone only if clinically needed. The pathway starts the VKA with the bridge within the safe window."},{"id":"D","text":"Begin the VKA together with therapeutic UFH or LMWH now","explanation":"The pathway recommends both within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe."}],"point":"Start a VKA with therapeutic UFH or LMWH within 24 hours or as soon as safe.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_bridge_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_start"],"answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking warfarin has a new drop in blood pressure. Which additional finding most directly supports bleeding as a contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New polyuria with clear urine","explanation":"A fluid loss may contribute to hypotension, but clear polyuria does not specifically point to hemorrhage."},{"id":"B","text":"New slow pulse after an AV-nodal blocker without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This could support a medication-related hemodynamic effect. It does not specifically support bleeding."},{"id":"C","text":"New fever and rigors without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This pattern may point toward infection rather than specifically toward bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"New melena or red urine","explanation":"These can reflect gastrointestinal or urinary bleeding. New hypotension increases urgency."}],"point":"Recognize urinary or gastrointestinal blood as a red flag with hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_occult_bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_occult_bleeding"],"answerKey":"D"}],"questionCount":9},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"advanced","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":true,"secondsPerQuestion":90,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"different from standard","questions":[{"stem":"During COUMADIN — warfarin therapy, a patient has new melena, pallor, dizziness, and a BP fall. Which concern best unifies the findings?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Clinically important blood loss","explanation":"The stool finding and hemodynamic change suggest bleeding with impaired perfusion."},{"id":"B","text":"A therapeutic reduction in coagulation alone","explanation":"Bleeding with circulatory symptoms is more than evidence of the intended drug action."},{"id":"C","text":"Uncomplicated postural intolerance","explanation":"This does not account for the new evidence of GI bleeding."},{"id":"D","text":"Isolated vasodilator headache","explanation":"This does not explain melena and the systemic blood-loss pattern."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Recognize important bleeding during antithrombotic treatment.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--bleeding","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::bleeding"],"answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"A mechanical-valve patient is prescribed parenteral bridging while warfarin is established. Which pharmacodynamic distinction explains the overlap?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Warfarin directly activates plasmin to lyse existing thrombi","explanation":"That is a fibrinolytic mechanism, not the VKA mechanism."},{"id":"B","text":"Warfarin reduces new factor synthesis while existing factors decline over time","explanation":"The full anticoagulant effect is delayed."},{"id":"C","text":"Warfarin irreversibly blocks platelet COX-1 before changing INR","explanation":"That describes aspirin’s primary platelet action."},{"id":"D","text":"Warfarin directly neutralizes circulating thrombin within seconds","explanation":"That is not warfarin’s action."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Warfarin’s full effect is delayed relative to administration.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--warfarin_delay","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_delay"],"answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"A warfarin-treated patient starts an interacting medicine or becomes acutely ill. Which follow-up most directly checks a changed anticoagulant response?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Pain score and joint movement","explanation":"These do not establish changed anticoagulation intensity."},{"id":"B","text":"SpO2 trend and inhaler technique","explanation":"These do not quantify the anticoagulant response."},{"id":"C","text":"QT interval and pulse-oximeter waveform","explanation":"These do not replace warfarin-specific monitoring."},{"id":"D","text":"INR trend and bleeding assessment","explanation":"Interaction or illness can alter warfarin response and bleeding risk."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Medicines, illness, and diet changes can alter warfarin response.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--warfarin_interactions","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_interactions"],"answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Two mechanical-valve patients have different prescribed INR targets. Which difference can justify this?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Oral versus parenteral administration of warfarin maintenance","explanation":"The maintenance is oral and the target is not established this way."},{"id":"B","text":"Morning versus evening administration of the same warfarin dose","explanation":"Administration time does not define the valve-related target."},{"id":"C","text":"Valve type or position and patient thromboembolic risk","explanation":"These inform the patient-specific target."},{"id":"D","text":"Presence versus absence of a pre-dose meal","explanation":"Meal timing is not the basis of the valve-specific target."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Mechanical-valve anticoagulation targets depend on valve and patient factors.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--mechanical_valve","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::mechanical_valve"],"answerKey":"C"},{"stem":"Why does a mechanical valve create a continuing indication for prescribed anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Artificial non-endothelial surfaces and disturbed flow promote thrombus formation","explanation":"Anticoagulation reduces valve thrombosis and embolic risk."},{"id":"B","text":"The prosthesis produces a permanent vitamin-K deficiency","explanation":"The thrombogenic surface, not vitamin-K deficiency, explains the indication."},{"id":"C","text":"The prosthesis requires routine dissolution of its structural material","explanation":"Anticoagulation does not dissolve the valve itself."},{"id":"D","text":"The prosthesis prevents circulating platelets from reaching the heart","explanation":"This is not the mechanism of valve-related thrombosis."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Artificial valve surfaces and disturbed flow promote thrombosis and embolism.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--advanced--valve_thrombosis","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::valve_thrombosis"],"answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":5},{"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day","itemId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin","itemType":"medication","title":"COUMADIN — warfarin","stage":"7-day","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"operator":">","percent":90,"roundBeforeComparison":false},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"persisted total deadline: 90 seconds multiplied by question count"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"show keys and all option explanations after submission","retestPolicy":"comprehensive memory retest; questions may repeat","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"What is the principal mechanism of action of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Relaxes prostate and urethral smooth muscle","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"B","text":"Inhibits bacterial cell-wall synthesis","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"C","text":"Changes inflammatory/stress-response gene transcription","explanation":"This is a different pharmacological action. The drug’s action is: VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."},{"id":"D","text":"Reduces vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis","explanation":"VKORC1 inhibition reduces regeneration of vitamin K and synthesis of functional clotting factors."}],"point":"Identify the principal pharmacological action taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::principal-action"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--principal-action","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which drug class does COUMADIN — warfarin belong to?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Unfractionated heparin","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"B","text":"Vitamin-K antagonist","explanation":"Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"C","text":"Antiplatelet","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."},{"id":"D","text":"Fibrinolytic","explanation":"The medicine is classified as: Vitamin K antagonist (VKA) oral anticoagulant."}],"point":"Identify the drug class taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::drug-class"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--drug-class","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which is a clinical use of COUMADIN — warfarin?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Replacement of glucose during severe hypoglycemia","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"B","text":"Relief of bronchospasm through airway receptor stimulation","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"C","text":"Suppression of bacterial protein synthesis","explanation":"This is a different treatment role."},{"id":"D","text":"Long-term anticoagulation for selected indications","explanation":"This is a clinical use of the medicine: Long-term oral anticoagulation for selected thromboembolic indications. A key CCU example is a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including mechanical aortic valve replacement."}],"point":"Identify the main treatment role taught for this medicine.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::treatment-role"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--treatment-role","answerKey":"D"},{"stem":"Which result is used to adjust warfarin against the prescribed therapeutic target?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"INR","explanation":"INR is the routine warfarin anticoagulation measure."},{"id":"B","text":"Serum potassium","explanation":"Potassium does not quantify warfarin effect."},{"id":"C","text":"Platelet count","explanation":"This measures cell quantity, not the warfarin anticoagulation intensity."},{"id":"D","text":"aPTT","explanation":"This is used for other coagulation assessments and some UFH protocols, not routine warfarin targeting."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Warfarin is monitored against an individually prescribed INR target.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_inr","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_inr"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_inr","answerKey":"A"},{"stem":"Which agent has a role in reversing warfarin’s effect under the appropriate clinical protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Naloxone","explanation":"It reverses opioid effects, not vitamin-K antagonism."},{"id":"B","text":"Atropine","explanation":"It treats selected bradycardia through muscarinic blockade."},{"id":"C","text":"Adenosine","explanation":"It transiently blocks AV-nodal conduction rather than reversing warfarin."},{"id":"D","text":"Vitamin K","explanation":"It supports restoration of vitamin-K-dependent factor synthesis; the full reversal plan depends on severity."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Vitamin K has an indication-specific role in warfarin reversal.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_reversal","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_reversal"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_reversal","answerKey":"D"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Warfarin and therapeutic heparin were started after mechanical-valve implantation. Today is the first day the INR reaches its prescribed range. Which finding supports stopping the bridge under the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"The aPTT reaches the heparin goal on two consecutive days.","explanation":"aPTT can reflect UFH effect, but does not establish sustained therapeutic VKA effect."},{"id":"B","text":"INR remains in the prescribed range on a second consecutive day.","explanation":"ESC/EACTS describes two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before stopping the bridge."},{"id":"C","text":"Two oral warfarin doses have been given and the INR is still rising.","explanation":"Number of doses and direction of change do not establish two days of therapeutic INR."},{"id":"D","text":"INR reaches the lower edge of its range on the first day.","explanation":"One in-range day does not meet this criterion."}],"point":"After mechanical-valve implantation, ESC/EACTS uses two consecutive days of therapeutic INR before ending the heparin bridge.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_bridge_stop","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_stop"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_bridge_stop","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient stable on warfarin plans to eat more leafy greens for health. What advice best supports stable anticoagulation?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Move leafy greens to the morning and take warfarin at night to avoid the effect.","explanation":"Separating the times does not remove the effect of vitamin K intake on INR."},{"id":"B","text":"Keep vitamin K intake consistent and discuss the planned change with the anticoagulation team.","explanation":"A stable intake supports stable INR. The team can advise on follow-up if intake changes."},{"id":"C","text":"Avoid leafy greens on every day that warfarin is scheduled.","explanation":"Routine avoidance is unnecessary. Consistency matters."},{"id":"D","text":"Offset extra leafy greens by taking an extra warfarin dose that evening.","explanation":"Self-adjusting warfarin to individual meals risks unstable anticoagulation."}],"point":"Keep dietary vitamin K consistent on warfarin.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_vitamin_k_consistency","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After mechanical-valve implantation, the team considers postoperative hemostasis adequate for anticoagulation at 18 hours. Which initiation plan matches the ESC/EACTS pathway?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Begin the VKA and prophylactic-dose heparin now","explanation":"The mechanical-valve bridge uses therapeutic anticoagulation when safe."},{"id":"B","text":"Begin therapeutic heparin now and postpone the VKA for a fixed week","explanation":"Postpone only if clinically needed. The pathway starts the VKA with the bridge within the safe window."},{"id":"C","text":"Begin the VKA together with therapeutic UFH or LMWH now","explanation":"The pathway recommends both within 24 hours or as soon as considered safe."},{"id":"D","text":"Begin the VKA now and add therapeutic heparin only once INR is in range","explanation":"The bridge covers the interval before a stable therapeutic INR. Waiting defeats that purpose."}],"point":"Start a VKA with therapeutic UFH or LMWH within 24 hours or as soon as safe.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--7-day--warfarin_bridge_start","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-warfarin-coumadin::warfarin_bridge_start"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-warfarin-coumadin--standard--warfarin_bridge_start","answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient taking warfarin has a new drop in blood pressure. Which additional finding most directly supports bleeding as a contributor?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"New slow pulse after an AV-nodal blocker without a change in stool or urine","explanation":"This could support a medication-related hemodynamic effect. It does not specifically support bleeding."},{"id":"B","text":"New polyuria with clear urine","explanation":"A fluid loss may contribute to hypotension, but clear polyuria does not specifically point to hemorrhage."},{"id":"C","text":"New melena or red urine","explanation":"These can reflect gastrointestinal or urinary bleeding. New hypotension increases urgency."},{"id":"D","text":"New fever and rigors without a change in stool or urine","explana