":"Increased cGMP promotes smooth-muscle relaxation, not constriction.","id":"A"},{"text":"NO raises cGMP and promotes venodilation, lowering preload and BP","explanation":"Increased cGMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Venodilation reduces venous return and can lower pressure.","id":"B"},{"text":"NO lowers cGMP and promotes venodilation, lowering preload and BP","explanation":"The hemodynamic direction matches but NO increases cGMP.","id":"C"},{"text":"NO lowers cGMP and promotes venoconstriction, raising preload and BP","explanation":"Neither the cGMP direction nor the vascular response fits nitrate pharmacology.","id":"D"}],"point":"Connect NO/cGMP-mediated vascular relaxation to reduced preload and blood pressure.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism","why"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pharmacologic class contains isosorbide mononitrate?","choices":[{"text":"Organic nitrate","explanation":"It produces nitric-oxide-mediated vasodilation.","id":"A"},{"text":"Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker","explanation":"That group reduces calcium entry through L-type channels.","id":"B"},{"text":"Beta-adrenergic blocker","explanation":"That group reduces adrenergic stimulation rather than delivering nitrate-mediated vasodilation.","id":"C"},{"text":"Late sodium current inhibitor","explanation":"That mechanism describes ranolazine, a different antianginal drug.","id":"D"}],"point":"Distinguish an organic nitrate from other antianginal classes.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with recurrent exertional angina needs an oral medicine to reduce episodes over time. Which is the intended role of ordered isosorbide mononitrate?","choices":[{"text":"An extra dose at symptom onset to terminate each episode rapidly","explanation":"The oral mononitrate formulation does not act quickly enough for rescue.","id":"A"},{"text":"A pre-procedure dose to replace prescribed antiplatelet protection","explanation":"Nitrates relieve ischemic symptoms through hemodynamic effects and do not inhibit platelets.","id":"B"},{"text":"Scheduled therapy to reduce the frequency of angina","explanation":"Oral mononitrate is intended for prevention and control.","id":"C"},{"text":"A short course to terminate coronary thrombosis","explanation":"Mononitrate does not dissolve thrombi.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify scheduled preventive use.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After an oral isosorbide mononitrate dose, which new symptom and observation pair best fits the expected vasodilatory adverse-effect pattern?","choices":[{"text":"Headache with sustained BP elevation","explanation":"Headache can occur, but sustained hypertension does not fit the expected vasodilatory BP effect.","id":"A"},{"text":"Headache and standing dizziness with a BP drop","explanation":"Headache and postural symptoms can accompany nitrate vasodilation. Recheck BP and assess severity.","id":"B"},{"text":"Dizziness with marked new AV block and unchanged BP","explanation":"A new conduction defect needs assessment. It is not the characteristic BP-mediated dizziness of nitrates.","id":"C"},{"text":"Dizziness with falling glucose and unchanged BP","explanation":"This suggests a separate metabolic cause.","id":"D"}],"point":"Link nitrate headache and postural symptoms to 