thesis inhibition","explanation":"Neither drug is acting as an antibacterial."},{"id":"B","text":"Excessive vasodilation and hypotension","explanation":"The combination can produce a dangerous pressure fall."},{"id":"C","text":"Excessive clotting-factor synthesis","explanation":"These drugs do not combine through vitamin-K-dependent coagulation."},{"id":"D","text":"Excessive gastric acid secretion","explanation":"This is not the clinically important interaction."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates can cause profound hypotension.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--nitrate_pde5","answerKey":"B"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient with sudden chest pain asks to use a maintenance isosorbide mononitrate tablet as rescue. Which distinction matters?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"An identical nitrate class establishing an identical onset","explanation":"Shared class does not make maintenance and rescue products equivalent."},{"id":"B","text":"Long-acting prevention versus the authorized acute chest-pain plan","explanation":"Maintenance oral mononitrate is not a rapid-rescue formulation."},{"id":"C","text":"The trade name alone rather than its formulation and intended role","explanation":"The formulation and indication are essential; a familiar name does not establish rapid rescue."},{"id":"D","text":"The tablet’s total milligrams establishing the speed of relief","explanation":"Dose size alone does not establish an appropriate rescue onset."}],"point":"Oral long-acting isosorbide mononitrate is preventive, not rapid rescue.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--nitrate_rescue","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::nitrate_rescue"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--nitrate_rescue","answerKey":"B"},{"stem":"The prescribed VASOCORE — isosorbide mononitrate and the supplied product differ in release type or route. What establishes whether substitution is acceptable?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Product-specific interchangeability and an authorized order","explanation":"Formulation differences require a defensible, authorized substitution."},{"id":"B","text":"A previously tolerated dose of the original formulation","explanation":"Prior tolerance does not establish equivalence of the new formulation."},{"id":"C","text":"A similar dosing interval listed on another patient’s chart","explanation":"Another regimen cannot establish product interchangeability."},{"id":"D","text":"An identical generic name and nominal milligram strength","explanation":"These do not establish the same release, route, or bioavailability."}],"format":"single-best-answer","point":"Verify product-specific interchangeability and authorization when route or release type differs.","criticalForSafety":false,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--7-day--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::formulation"],"reusedFromQuestionId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate--advanced--formulation","answerKey":"A"}],"questionCount":8}],"scoringExclusions":[{"learningPointId":"ccu-isosorbide-mononitrate::hypotension","action":"omit","reason":"Context-free shared template: the answer is supplied by the symptom or asks for a general product-reference rule. Omit from drug-specific scoring; do not credit this as coverage of the underlying drug-specific administration or BP fact.","unresolvedCoverage":true}]},{"itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","itemType":"medication","capabilityRequirement":null,"exams":[{"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive","itemId":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate","itemType":"medication","title":"CORDIL — isosorbide dinitrate","stage":"comprehensive","reviewStatus":"authoring draft","passRule":{"type":"error-count","allowedErrors":1},"timing":{"timed":false,"secondsPerQuestion":null,"timerEnforcement":"untimed"},"scoring":{"correct":1,"incorrect":0,"unanswered":0,"partialCredit":false,"unsureOrGuessed":"review flag only","criticalSafetyMiss":"separate follow-up required; original numeric score unchanged"},"feedback":"incorrect, unanswered and unsure/guessed questions only after submission","retestPolicy":"same comprehensive exam and questions at every stage","questions":[{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which signaling and vascular effect pair accounts for isosorbide dinitrate action?","choices":[{"text":"Increased nitric oxide signaling lowers cGMP and contracts vascular smooth muscle","explanation":"NO activates guanylate cyclase and raises cGMP.","id":"A"},{"text":"Increased nitric oxide signaling raises cGMP and relaxes vascular smooth muscle","explanation":"Organic nitrates increase NO signaling and cGMP, leading to relaxation.","id":"B"},{"text":"Increased beta1 signaling raises cAMP and directly relaxes veins","explanation":"Beta1 signaling is not the primary pathway for nitrate-induced vasodilation.","id":"C"},{"text":"Increased alpha1 signaling raises intracellular calcium and contracts vascular smooth muscle","explanation":"This describes vasoconstriction, the opposite of the nitrate effect.","id":"D"}],"point":"Identify nitric-oxide/cGMP-mediated vascular smooth muscle relaxation.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--principal-action","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::principal-action"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["mechanism"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which pharmacologic class contains isosorbide dinitrate?","choices":[{"text":"Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker","explanation":"That group reduces calcium entry through L-type channels.","id":"A"},{"text":"Organic nitrate","explanation":"It produces nitric-oxide-mediated vasodilation.","id":"B"},{"text":"Beta-adrenergic blocker","explanation":"That group reduces adrenergic stimulation rather than delivering nitrate-mediated vasodilation.","id":"C"},{"text":"Late sodium current inhibitor","explanation":"That mechanism describes ranolazine, a different antianginal drug.","id":"D"}],"point":"Distinguish an organic nitrate from other antianginal classes.","criticalForSafety":false,"answerKey":"B","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--drug-class","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::drug-class"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["drugclass"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which relative onset should the nurse expect from verified sublingual isosorbide dinitrate compared with oral maintenance isosorbide mononitrate?","choices":[{"text":"Oral maintenance mononitrate starts sooner because it is already the active metabolite","explanation":"Metabolite status does not make an oral preventive tablet suitable for rapid rescue.","id":"A"},{"text":"Equivalent nitrate activity means the two route formulations have the same onset","explanation":"Nitrate class does not establish route-specific onset.","id":"B"},{"text":"The higher labeled tablet strength always predicts the faster onset","explanation":"Milligrams cannot establish onset between different drugs or routes.","id":"C"},{"text":"Sublingual dinitrate can start acting sooner than oral maintenance mononitrate","explanation":"The formulations serve different onset goals. Follow the prescribed acute chest-pain plan. Sublingual nitroglycerin generally acts faster than sublingual dinitrate.","id":"D"}],"point":"Distinguish onset by route and formulation without implying preferred acute rescue.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--treatment-role","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::treatment-role"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["use"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"After sublingual isosorbide dinitrate, which cluster is consistent with the nitrate’s vasodilatory effect and indicates why BP should be reassessed?","choices":[{"text":"Headache, facial pallor and dizziness with persistently rising BP","explanation":"The rising BP does not fit the characteristic nitrate vasodilatory effect.","id":"A"},{"text":"Flushing and dizziness with falling glucose but unchanged BP","explanation":"Hypoglycemia needs assessment but is not the expected primary mechanism.","id":"B"},{"text":"Dizziness with progressive AV block and unchanged BP","explanation":"A conduction disturbance needs assessment. It does not describe the typical vasodilatory pattern.","id":"C"},{"text":"Headache, facial flushing and dizziness with lower BP","explanation":"Vasodilation can produce all three symptoms and lower arterial pressure.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recognize headache, flushing, dizziness and hypotension after nitrate delivery.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"D","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--nitrate_headache","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::nitrate_headache"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient’s order specifies the 5-mg isosorbide dinitrate tablet listed in the Palestinian formulary for the faster-acting nitrate route. Which placement matches that specific formulation?","choices":[{"text":"Let the tablet dissolve under the tongue","explanation":"This matches the prescribed sublingual route.","id":"A"},{"text":"Let the tablet dissolve between the cheek and gum","explanation":"This is buccal placement, a different route.","id":"B"},{"text":"Swallow the intact tablet with water","explanation":"Swallowing changes the delivery route of an SL tablet.","id":"C"},{"text":"Crush the tablet into an oral liquid and swallow it","explanation":"This would deliver the drug enterally rather than sublingually.","id":"D"}],"point":"Recall the verified sublingual route.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--sl_tablet","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::sl_tablet"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["administration"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"Which clinical scenario can justify a specifically ordered controlled IV nitrate under a monitored protocol?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Isolated bradycardia with hypotension and no ischemic symptoms","explanation":"An IV nitrate does not provide chronotropic or pressor support."},{"id":"B","text":"Cardiogenic shock with markedly low pressure and impaired perfusion","explanation":"Additional nitrate vasodilation can worsen hypotension in this setting."},{"id":"C","text":"Persistent severe ischemic chest pain or refractory acute left-sided heart failure with adequate pressure","explanation":"A monitored IV nitrate can be considered in these selected settings when authorized and hemodynamically appropriate."},{"id":"D","text":"Stable exertional angina without symptoms and a need for routine maintenance","explanation":"Routine stable maintenance is not an indication for an urgent IV infusion."}],"point":"Select severe or unstable angina or refractory LV failure as IV nitrate contexts.","criticalForSafety":true,"id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--isdn_iv_context","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::isdn_iv_context"],"answerKey":"C"},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A patient took sildenafil before a planned isosorbide dinitrate dose. Which interaction mechanism explains the immediate safety concern?","choices":[{"text":"Increased cGMP breakdown reduces nitrate absorption","explanation":"PDE5 inhibition slows cGMP breakdown. This is not an absorption interaction.","id":"A"},{"text":"Platelet COX-1 inhibition amplifies nitrate-related bleeding","explanation":"The characteristic interaction is hypotension, not additive COX-1 inhibition.","id":"B"},{"text":"Reduced cGMP breakdown amplifies nitrate vasodilation","explanation":"Sildenafil can markedly increase nitrate-induced hypotension. Escalate the interaction before administration.","id":"C"},{"text":"Increased AV nodal blockade combines to cause bradycardia","explanation":"The concern is amplified vascular relaxation, rather than direct AV nodal blockade.","id":"D"}],"point":"Link PDE5 blockade with amplified nitrate cGMP effects.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"C","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--nitrate_pde5","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate::nitrate_pde5"],"revision":"drug-dependent-20261106","sourceFields":["monitor"]},{"format":"single-best-answer","stem":"A prescriber orders an IV nitrate infusion, and the only confirmed isosorbide dinitrate product on the trolley is a sublingual tablet. What preparation would fulfill the IV order?","choices":[{"text":"An exact IV-labeled product with verified concentration, compatible tubing and infusion method","explanation":"A separate IV formulation is needed. Product and material choice can affect delivery.","id":"A"},{"text":"The SL tablet dissolved in IV fluid with the same labeled mass","explanation":"The SL tablet is not a sterile IV dosage form and cannot fulfill the infusion order.","id":"B"},{"text":"An IV nitrate borrowed from another order with the same pump rate","explanation":"Different nitrate preparations and concentrations make copying a rate unsafe.","id":"C"},{"text":"An IV vial labeled only with the same trade name, using the SL tablet strength to set the rate","explanation":"The trade name and tablet strength cannot establish the IV preparation or rate.","id":"D"}],"point":"Require a separate product labeled for IV use with verified administration requirements.","criticalForSafety":true,"answerKey":"A","id":"ccu-isosorbide-dinitrate--comprehensive--formulation","points":1,"learningPointIds":["ccu-isosorbide-dinitrat